WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.069 --> 00:00:03.360
MATTHEW SPALDING: Good evening.

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:05.440
I'm Matthew Spalding,
Associate Vice President

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:07.830
Dean of Educational Programs
for Hillsdale College

00:00:07.830 --> 00:00:11.100
here in Washington, D.C. Welcome
to the Allan P. Kirby Center

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:13.770
for Constitutional
Studies and Citizenship,

00:00:13.770 --> 00:00:16.506
Hillsdale East as
we like to call it.

00:00:16.506 --> 00:00:17.880
And I'm very
pleased this evening

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:22.180
to welcome a good friend
of mine and of the college.

00:00:22.180 --> 00:00:23.880
Our students have
left for the semester,

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:26.787
although I'm currently
grading their exams,

00:00:26.787 --> 00:00:28.620
and the summer students
haven't arrived yet,

00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:33.600
so you'll have to settle
with my introduction.

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:37.870
I'm especially pleased to
have him here for two reasons.

00:00:37.870 --> 00:00:43.470
One is, as a college,
we have proudly

00:00:43.470 --> 00:00:48.300
continued to teach
things like the classics.

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:50.420
And our speaker this
evening is a great scholar

00:00:50.420 --> 00:00:54.390
of the classics, someone
whose books I hope all of us

00:00:54.390 --> 00:00:55.920
have enjoyed.

00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:59.770
But also, as you'll
hear in a moment,

00:00:59.770 --> 00:01:03.353
we share some common roots
in terms of where we grew up.

00:01:03.353 --> 00:01:05.519
Victor Davis Hanson is a
senior fellow at the Hoover

00:01:05.519 --> 00:01:09.300
Institution and a professor of
classics emeritus at California

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:11.760
State University Fresno.

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:15.090
He's also, we're happy to
say, the Wayne &amp; Marcia Buske

00:01:15.090 --> 00:01:18.064
Distinguished Fellow in
History at Hillsdale College.

00:01:18.064 --> 00:01:20.730
He received his B.A. in classics
at the University of California

00:01:20.730 --> 00:01:23.640
Santa Cruz, is a fellow at the
American School of Classical

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:26.580
Studies in Athens, and
received his PhD in classics

00:01:26.580 --> 00:01:29.160
from Stanford University.

00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:31.320
He is from California.

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:34.200
Indeed, he was the fifth
successive generation

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:36.480
to live in the same
house on his family farm

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:39.780
in Selma, California,
which is in the Central

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:42.810
Valley of California,
which is about 45,

00:01:42.810 --> 00:01:45.510
40 minutes north of where I
grew up in a little town called

00:01:45.510 --> 00:01:48.360
Corcoran.

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:53.700
And that fact explains a
lot of his understanding

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:56.399
and his shaping of how he
thinks about many things,

00:01:56.399 --> 00:01:56.940
as we'll see.

00:01:58.690 --> 00:02:02.110
He was a full time orchard
and vineyard grower right

00:02:02.110 --> 00:02:04.840
after he got his PhD, so I'm
glad to see that he put it

00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:09.370
to good use before he went
to CSU Fresno's campus

00:02:09.370 --> 00:02:12.690
to initiate their
classics language program.

00:02:12.690 --> 00:02:14.940
In 2000, he was awarded the
National Humanities Medal.

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:17.610
In 2008, he won
the Bradley Prize.

00:02:17.610 --> 00:02:20.370
He's a weekly columnist for
National Review online, as well

00:02:20.370 --> 00:02:22.590
as the Tribune Media Services.

00:02:22.590 --> 00:02:26.820
He's published several
journals, newspapers, magazines.

00:02:26.820 --> 00:02:30.210
He's actually one of those
people I oftentimes looked down

00:02:30.210 --> 00:02:32.250
upon, detest, am jealous of.

00:02:32.250 --> 00:02:37.449
He's written 23 books, including
Wars of the Ancient Greeks

00:02:37.449 --> 00:02:38.490
and A War Like No Other--

00:02:38.490 --> 00:02:42.300
How the Athenians and Spartans
Fought the Peloponnesian War.

00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:45.300
His most recent work will be
released in October, The Second

00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:46.020
World Wars--

00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:51.590
How the First Global
Conflict was Fought and Won.

00:02:51.590 --> 00:02:54.266
As the Distinguished Fellow
in History at Hillsdale,

00:02:54.266 --> 00:02:55.640
he regularly
teaches our students

00:02:55.640 --> 00:03:00.020
on campus about military
history and classroom culture.

00:03:00.020 --> 00:03:02.220
He also teaches in
our online courses,

00:03:02.220 --> 00:03:05.900
if you're interested, which
are all free at hillsdale.edu.

00:03:05.900 --> 00:03:08.870
And he was involved in our
course on Athens and Sparta,

00:03:08.870 --> 00:03:10.566
which came out last spring.

00:03:10.566 --> 00:03:12.440
And he taught sessions
on life and government

00:03:12.440 --> 00:03:15.350
in Athens, Athens
in the Persian War,

00:03:15.350 --> 00:03:18.530
and Athens in the
Peloponnesian War.

00:03:18.530 --> 00:03:21.800
He's recently been writing about
the current election, what's

00:03:21.800 --> 00:03:23.750
gone on in the United
States, Donald Trump

00:03:23.750 --> 00:03:26.390
and Trumpism, foreign
policy challenges, questions

00:03:26.390 --> 00:03:28.880
of immigration.

00:03:28.880 --> 00:03:30.590
His talk this
evening is entitled

00:03:30.590 --> 00:03:33.870
Taking Stock of Trumpism,
Where It Came From,

00:03:33.870 --> 00:03:36.230
What He's Accomplished,
and Where He's Going.

00:03:36.230 --> 00:03:38.367
Please join me in welcoming
our guest this evening,

00:03:38.367 --> 00:03:39.200
Victor Davis Hanson.

00:03:39.200 --> 00:03:51.676
[APPLAUSE]

00:03:51.676 --> 00:03:53.800
VICTOR DAVIS HANSON: There's
only 5,000 classicists

00:03:53.800 --> 00:03:55.841
in the United States, so
it's a very small group.

00:03:55.841 --> 00:03:59.680
I mention that because
I got a letter yesterday

00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:01.780
from a very angry
classicist, and he

00:04:01.780 --> 00:04:06.460
said, "You've become the
Enoch Powell of classics,

00:04:06.460 --> 00:04:08.990
and you haven't done anything
good til you wrote that one

00:04:08.990 --> 00:04:10.910
good article on
the use of numbers

00:04:10.910 --> 00:04:12.850
in Thucydides' seventh book.

00:04:12.850 --> 00:04:15.460
Ever since then, it's
all been downhill,

00:04:15.460 --> 00:04:18.329
and you'll regret it
on your deathbed."

00:04:18.329 --> 00:04:20.380
That's the classi- Reminded
me of what Churchill

00:04:20.380 --> 00:04:24.310
said about classicists: "They
know little Latin and less

00:04:24.310 --> 00:04:27.460
Greek, and yet they're
supposed to be experts on it."

00:04:27.460 --> 00:04:30.790
But I've had a rocky
relationship in the world

00:04:30.790 --> 00:04:32.380
of classical languages.

00:04:32.380 --> 00:04:34.930
I thought I would just
speak for about 30 minutes

00:04:34.930 --> 00:04:38.650
on the phenomenon of
Trumpsim, where he came from,

00:04:38.650 --> 00:04:40.510
how he got elected,
what he's trying to do,

00:04:40.510 --> 00:04:44.110
and what the future holds.

00:04:44.110 --> 00:04:47.060
All of you have come
across that phenomenon

00:04:47.060 --> 00:04:49.449
or maybe you
participated in when

00:04:49.449 --> 00:04:51.490
you had to look both ways
when somebody asked you

00:04:51.490 --> 00:04:53.020
if you voted for Trump.

00:04:53.020 --> 00:04:54.850
Or you always said this phrase--

00:04:54.850 --> 00:04:59.200
this is my favorite-- "well,
he wasn't my first choice."

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:03.340
Or after the primary, "I don't
agree with everything he does,"

00:05:03.340 --> 00:05:04.925
but you hear that a lot.

00:05:04.925 --> 00:05:05.800
It's always the same.

00:05:08.530 --> 00:05:12.820
Well, Hillary Clinton the
other day gave an interview,

00:05:12.820 --> 00:05:15.839
actually she gave a speech to
a women's international group,

00:05:15.839 --> 00:05:17.880
in which she said she was
robbed of the election.

00:05:17.880 --> 00:05:21.160
She said, typical Hillary,
I take full responsibility,

00:05:21.160 --> 00:05:26.020
but I was robbed because
I'm a woman, of course,

00:05:26.020 --> 00:05:28.900
and of course, James
Comey stole the election.

00:05:28.900 --> 00:05:32.410
You think that they'd be
mad that he was still there

00:05:32.410 --> 00:05:34.510
rather than mad that
he was fired yesterday.

00:05:34.510 --> 00:05:39.350
But, nevertheless, and then of
course, the Russians did it.

00:05:39.350 --> 00:05:41.150
As I said to a group
today, the Russians

00:05:41.150 --> 00:05:48.110
did it is sort of like Goring
going to Hitler in August 1941

00:05:48.110 --> 00:05:50.510
and said, well we could
have taken Moscow,

00:05:50.510 --> 00:05:52.580
but it was very cold.

00:05:52.580 --> 00:05:56.750
And Methuen said it's not
like we're a tropical people.

00:05:56.750 --> 00:05:58.280
In the German
military handbook it

00:05:58.280 --> 00:06:00.820
said how to prepare
for snow, but they

00:06:00.820 --> 00:06:02.540
were blaming
everything on the snow,

00:06:02.540 --> 00:06:04.310
as if the Red Army
didn't have a role

00:06:04.310 --> 00:06:05.705
in their defeat outside Moscow.

00:06:08.561 --> 00:06:09.560
That's what Russians do.

00:06:09.560 --> 00:06:10.910
They try to interfere
with elections.

00:06:10.910 --> 00:06:12.285
They didn't put
a gun to her head

00:06:12.285 --> 00:06:17.780
and say have a home
brew server or approve

00:06:17.780 --> 00:06:19.580
the sale of one quarter
of uranium holdings

00:06:19.580 --> 00:06:20.480
in North America.

00:06:20.480 --> 00:06:22.610
But she's still angry.

00:06:22.610 --> 00:06:25.520
And what lost her, of course,
that election and empowered

00:06:25.520 --> 00:06:29.150
Trump was that she was
shrill, snarky, she

00:06:29.150 --> 00:06:30.260
wasn't a good candidate.

00:06:34.840 --> 00:06:40.720
When she told the truth, she
was less believable than when

00:06:40.720 --> 00:06:41.760
Trump told a lie.

00:06:44.690 --> 00:06:48.770
And she was a year
younger than Trump,

00:06:48.770 --> 00:06:52.820
and physically she was 10
years older than Trump.

00:06:52.820 --> 00:06:57.290
And Trump violated all the
rules of personal fitness.

00:06:57.290 --> 00:06:58.666
He looked too heavy.

00:06:58.666 --> 00:07:01.040
I had a friend that met him,
and I said what was it like,

00:07:01.040 --> 00:07:05.450
and he said I got a choice
between McDonald's Big Mac

00:07:05.450 --> 00:07:07.820
and Burger King.

00:07:07.820 --> 00:07:11.300
And he said then for dessert it
was Haagen Dazs out of the cup.

00:07:11.300 --> 00:07:14.390
So Hillary ate right, and
she did everything right,

00:07:14.390 --> 00:07:17.300
and that she was less healthy
than somebody who was older

00:07:17.300 --> 00:07:20.540
and had that animal
cunning and animal energy.

00:07:20.540 --> 00:07:23.000
So she wasn't a
strong candidate.

00:07:23.000 --> 00:07:27.170
She had no message
other than I'm a woman,

00:07:27.170 --> 00:07:30.200
and I'm not Donald
Trump, the monster.

00:07:30.200 --> 00:07:33.890
And then, the only
key to her election

00:07:33.890 --> 00:07:37.640
is that she was going
to win only one way.

00:07:37.640 --> 00:07:39.080
You knew it; we all knew it.

00:07:39.080 --> 00:07:43.460
That Barack Obama had
bequeathed a suicide pact.

00:07:43.460 --> 00:07:45.080
I should say, it's
a long metaphor,

00:07:45.080 --> 00:07:48.422
he'd signed a suicide pact
with the Democratic Party.

00:07:48.422 --> 00:07:49.880
Because basically
he said I'm going

00:07:49.880 --> 00:07:54.020
to go down the community
organizing identity politics

00:07:54.020 --> 00:07:57.010
Reverend Wright road
once I'm elected.

00:07:57.010 --> 00:08:01.250
And I'm going to get empower
identity politics minority

00:08:01.250 --> 00:08:04.580
groups to come out
in unheard of numbers

00:08:04.580 --> 00:08:07.070
and vote in mass at
levels you can't imagine--

00:08:07.070 --> 00:08:12.380
92%-- and whereas
the clinger vote,

00:08:12.380 --> 00:08:15.070
or what we now know as the
irredeemables and deplorables,

00:08:15.070 --> 00:08:18.170
will fall off, I will so
appeal to the other groups

00:08:18.170 --> 00:08:19.460
that it won't matter.

00:08:19.460 --> 00:08:21.890
And of course it was
true, but the problem

00:08:21.890 --> 00:08:25.880
was it was transferable to
nobody other than Barack Obama.

00:08:25.880 --> 00:08:27.680
So Hillary inherited
all the downside

00:08:27.680 --> 00:08:29.730
and none of the upside.

00:08:29.730 --> 00:08:32.570
And when Obama was
through with that carnage,

00:08:32.570 --> 00:08:34.880
he had ruined the
Democratic Party,

00:08:34.880 --> 00:08:38.780
and they had lost local
elections, governorships,

00:08:38.780 --> 00:08:40.450
state legislatures,
Senate, House,

00:08:40.450 --> 00:08:42.049
a generation of
the Supreme Court

00:08:42.049 --> 00:08:44.030
probably, and the presidency.

00:08:44.030 --> 00:08:46.760
And yet she doubled
down on that.

00:08:46.760 --> 00:08:49.160
She called one quarter
of the United States

00:08:49.160 --> 00:08:50.660
basically irredeemable
when she said

00:08:50.660 --> 00:08:52.820
half his supporters were such.

00:08:52.820 --> 00:08:56.450
And you thought,
if she was smart,

00:08:56.450 --> 00:08:58.520
she would reprise that
Annie Oakley role.

00:08:58.520 --> 00:09:00.290
It was pretty effective in 2008.

00:09:00.290 --> 00:09:03.350
Remember, she said that
she shot guns with her dad.

00:09:03.350 --> 00:09:04.400
She had Boilermakers.

00:09:04.400 --> 00:09:06.440
She bowled.

00:09:06.440 --> 00:09:09.350
But then you start to
realize that by 2016, it

00:09:09.350 --> 00:09:10.400
was a different party.

00:09:10.400 --> 00:09:12.260
Obama had destroyed
that Bill Clinton

00:09:12.260 --> 00:09:16.010
party, that aw shucks, good
old boy didn't exist anymore,

00:09:16.010 --> 00:09:20.184
and she inherited a paradigm
that would only work for Obama.

00:09:20.184 --> 00:09:22.100
And I think I wrote an
article saying you know

00:09:22.100 --> 00:09:23.266
where this is going to lead.

00:09:23.266 --> 00:09:26.150
It's going to lead to
very lucrative speaking

00:09:26.150 --> 00:09:29.180
engagements for Obama as
a reward for destroying

00:09:29.180 --> 00:09:29.900
his own party.

00:09:29.900 --> 00:09:31.441
He's going to be
loved, and she she's

00:09:31.441 --> 00:09:35.540
going to be hated for
trying to imitate something

00:09:35.540 --> 00:09:36.770
that has to be losing.

00:09:36.770 --> 00:09:39.890
And that's exactly why
she lost the election.

00:09:39.890 --> 00:09:42.590
And then, of course,
the other reason

00:09:42.590 --> 00:09:47.710
was that we all thought
Donald Trump had a say in it.

00:09:47.710 --> 00:09:50.414
We all thought that,
February, March,

00:09:50.414 --> 00:09:52.080
Donald Trump was the
only candidate that

00:09:52.080 --> 00:09:53.330
could lose to Hillary Clinton.

00:09:53.330 --> 00:09:54.360
Remember that?

00:09:54.360 --> 00:09:57.630
Everybody said, well
a lot of them did,

00:09:57.630 --> 00:10:01.200
Rubio is the ideal
candidate or Walker.

00:10:01.200 --> 00:10:02.220
We had good governors--

00:10:02.220 --> 00:10:04.410
Jindal, Christie, Walker.

00:10:04.410 --> 00:10:08.160
We had senators-- Rand
Paul and we had Jeb Bush.

00:10:08.160 --> 00:10:10.350
They had the dynasty,
the money, the name.

00:10:10.350 --> 00:10:14.490
We had outsiders that
were more versed in facts,

00:10:14.490 --> 00:10:18.870
like Fiorina and Ben Carson.

00:10:18.870 --> 00:10:21.731
Nobody thought Trump has
these particular gifts,

00:10:21.731 --> 00:10:23.730
but now looking back, I
think most of you agree,

00:10:23.730 --> 00:10:26.610
he might have been the only
candidate that could have won.

00:10:26.610 --> 00:10:28.470
Because unlike John
McCain, he was not

00:10:28.470 --> 00:10:30.510
going to put Reverend
Wright, that type,

00:10:30.510 --> 00:10:32.550
I'm not going to
mention Reverend Wright.

00:10:32.550 --> 00:10:36.480
And unlike Mitt
Romney, he wasn't

00:10:36.480 --> 00:10:38.370
going to apologize for
his station in life.

00:10:38.370 --> 00:10:42.320
You can imagine if
Trump had a run in 2008.

00:10:42.320 --> 00:10:45.900
Reverend Wright would have
been 24/7, all the time.

00:10:45.900 --> 00:10:48.600
And had he been
Mitt Romney in 2012

00:10:48.600 --> 00:10:51.240
when Candy Crowley
hijacked the--

00:10:51.240 --> 00:10:53.490
he would have jumped
over and grabbed the mic.

00:10:53.490 --> 00:10:57.410
So at least he was the first
person who when Lee Atwater--

00:10:57.410 --> 00:10:58.860
remember Lee Atwater?

00:10:58.860 --> 00:11:04.440
He gave us the Boston Harbor
tank and Willie Horton ads

00:11:04.440 --> 00:11:08.040
and turned the patrician,
aristocratic, snarky George

00:11:08.040 --> 00:11:13.570
H.W. Bush to somebody who ate
corn nuts and was one of us.

00:11:13.570 --> 00:11:15.150
And it was very effective.

00:11:15.150 --> 00:11:17.040
And after he died, Bush lost.

00:11:17.040 --> 00:11:19.680
But we hadn't seen
that type of guerrilla

00:11:19.680 --> 00:11:22.050
fighting until Trump came.

00:11:22.050 --> 00:11:24.850
And so whatever was
going to happen,

00:11:24.850 --> 00:11:27.360
he was going to take, for
once and all, the fight

00:11:27.360 --> 00:11:30.960
to Hillary in a very
mean-spirited but necessary

00:11:30.960 --> 00:11:31.830
way.

00:11:31.830 --> 00:11:34.050
And that's why he
would resonate in a way

00:11:34.050 --> 00:11:36.570
that she couldn't imagine.

00:11:36.570 --> 00:11:38.920
And he also understood
a couple of things.

00:11:38.920 --> 00:11:41.460
We've all been told that
demography is destiny,

00:11:41.460 --> 00:11:44.580
but is it really in
the electoral college?

00:11:44.580 --> 00:11:47.940
Who cares who wins the popular
vote if certain states are

00:11:47.940 --> 00:11:49.560
more important than others.

00:11:49.560 --> 00:11:51.910
A vote in Iowa was
worth a lot more,

00:11:51.910 --> 00:11:55.200
or Ohio or Wisconsin, a lot
more than my vote in California,

00:11:55.200 --> 00:11:57.390
at least on the national level.

00:11:57.390 --> 00:11:58.890
And Trump seemed to--

00:11:58.890 --> 00:12:05.010
I remember watching CNN
when somebody said--

00:12:05.010 --> 00:12:07.500
I think they were talking
about Jorge Ramos--

00:12:07.500 --> 00:12:10.560
he seems to be oblivious to
the ramifications of the Latino

00:12:10.560 --> 00:12:11.590
vote.

00:12:11.590 --> 00:12:13.590
I said yeah, he is.

00:12:13.590 --> 00:12:17.220
Because most of the Latino
vote, and I'm living somewhere

00:12:17.220 --> 00:12:20.670
that's 90% Mexican-American,
was going to go to two states--

00:12:20.670 --> 00:12:23.760
California, that was never
going to go red and Texas that

00:12:23.760 --> 00:12:25.530
was never going to go blue.

00:12:25.530 --> 00:12:27.212
And the states that
probably had turned,

00:12:27.212 --> 00:12:28.920
maybe Colorado and
New Mexico, were going

00:12:28.920 --> 00:12:30.655
to be turned no matter who.

00:12:30.655 --> 00:12:32.280
Now if he had made
the argument there's

00:12:32.280 --> 00:12:34.410
a half a million
Latinos who hate Trump

00:12:34.410 --> 00:12:36.510
in Ohio and Michigan
and Wisconsin,

00:12:36.510 --> 00:12:38.340
I would have listened to it.

00:12:38.340 --> 00:12:40.290
But the point I'm
making is that he

00:12:40.290 --> 00:12:44.040
looked at the electoral
college, and he

00:12:44.040 --> 00:12:47.400
understood that
demography is destiny

00:12:47.400 --> 00:12:50.040
as it applies to
particular states and not

00:12:50.040 --> 00:12:51.660
the popular vote.

00:12:51.660 --> 00:12:56.520
And then when you add one
final element to the election,

00:12:56.520 --> 00:13:00.710
that Trump campaigned as
if he was going to lose

00:13:00.710 --> 00:13:02.750
and he was desperate,
and Hillary campaigned

00:13:02.750 --> 00:13:04.080
as if she has already won.

00:13:04.080 --> 00:13:06.470
That was a classic
hare and the tortoise.

00:13:06.470 --> 00:13:11.690
And so she was down in places
like Georgia and Arizona

00:13:11.690 --> 00:13:14.540
that were never going to go
red, at least in our generation,

00:13:14.540 --> 00:13:16.440
thinking that I'm
going to get a mandate,

00:13:16.440 --> 00:13:19.700
while not visiting
Wisconsin once.

00:13:19.700 --> 00:13:20.900
And he had a certain touch.

00:13:20.900 --> 00:13:24.170
They made a strategic
error, the Clintons did,

00:13:24.170 --> 00:13:26.990
by thinking he is a
billionaire therefore

00:13:26.990 --> 00:13:28.220
he can't be a populist.

00:13:28.220 --> 00:13:30.110
You can be a populist
and be a trillionaire,

00:13:30.110 --> 00:13:32.840
and you can be dead
broke and be an elitist.

00:13:32.840 --> 00:13:35.160
And she's proved that very
well, she and her husband.

00:13:35.160 --> 00:13:37.100
And so are the Obamas.

00:13:37.100 --> 00:13:39.830
And so when he had
that Queens accent,

00:13:39.830 --> 00:13:43.120
and he surrounded themselves
with the likes of Mike Tyson,

00:13:43.120 --> 00:13:46.010
the World Wrestling
Federation people, people

00:13:46.010 --> 00:13:48.500
in these key electoral
states that I've got somebody

00:13:48.500 --> 00:13:52.010
like Donald Trump in my family,
and I bring him out every once

00:13:52.010 --> 00:13:53.360
in a while--

00:13:53.360 --> 00:13:55.070
I like him, but I
don't want to be seen--

00:13:55.070 --> 00:13:56.900
but they understood who he was.

00:13:56.900 --> 00:13:59.836
And that Queens accent
worked to his advantage.

00:13:59.836 --> 00:14:00.960
And I'm being very cynical.

00:14:00.960 --> 00:14:02.720
I don't want to
be snarky, myself.

00:14:02.720 --> 00:14:06.670
Because I talked to a
New York developer once.

00:14:06.670 --> 00:14:10.460
When he called me and said
I watch from my tower--

00:14:10.460 --> 00:14:11.807
he has a big high rise.

00:14:11.807 --> 00:14:12.890
I shouldn't say high rise.

00:14:12.890 --> 00:14:14.000
I guess it's a skyscraper.

00:14:14.000 --> 00:14:17.000
And he said I see Donald Trump.

00:14:17.000 --> 00:14:19.942
And it is true that he
deviates from his planned walk

00:14:19.942 --> 00:14:21.650
when he gets out of
his limo, and he goes

00:14:21.650 --> 00:14:23.450
and talks to cement people.

00:14:23.450 --> 00:14:26.010
And I see people clapping
that are on construction sites

00:14:26.010 --> 00:14:30.080
so whatever he is, it's
genuine that people like him.

00:14:30.080 --> 00:14:32.930
And I thought about that when
Hillary was telling the coal

00:14:32.930 --> 00:14:36.050
miners they had to learn
how to build solar panels,

00:14:36.050 --> 00:14:38.667
he was using the first
person plural pronoun "our."

00:14:38.667 --> 00:14:39.500
Did you notice that?

00:14:39.500 --> 00:14:41.124
I've never heard a
candidate in history

00:14:41.124 --> 00:14:44.630
say our miners, our
farmers, our soldiers.

00:14:44.630 --> 00:14:46.410
Nobody told him to do that.

00:14:46.410 --> 00:14:48.950
And then when you threw
Kelly Conway and Steve Bannon

00:14:48.950 --> 00:14:51.140
into the mix, these
are people that

00:14:51.140 --> 00:14:53.584
look like they've been
around the block, and Robby

00:14:53.584 --> 00:14:55.250
Mook and all those
guys that for Clinton

00:14:55.250 --> 00:14:58.490
look like they came right of
DuPont Circle, Upper West Side.

00:14:58.490 --> 00:15:01.190
So it was sort of the
metrosexuals against the guys

00:15:01.190 --> 00:15:02.900
who had been around too long.

00:15:02.900 --> 00:15:09.980
And over the years,
I've met, infrequently

00:15:09.980 --> 00:15:12.980
but on occasion, Steve Bannon,
so as I was saying to my wife,

00:15:12.980 --> 00:15:14.690
she goes who is
that guy, and as she

00:15:14.690 --> 00:15:17.450
said that, "You Can't
Always Get What You Want"

00:15:17.450 --> 00:15:20.010
started playing at a
right wing Trump rally.

00:15:20.010 --> 00:15:22.130
And I said that is Steve Bannon.

00:15:22.130 --> 00:15:25.790
So there were elements
that explain why Trump won.

00:15:25.790 --> 00:15:28.730
I want to add, if I could,
just a little excursus.

00:15:28.730 --> 00:15:31.940
There was also a
deep-seated anger,

00:15:31.940 --> 00:15:35.600
emotional in America that can't
really be articulated well

00:15:35.600 --> 00:15:38.141
because it's an
emotional gut felt.

00:15:38.141 --> 00:15:39.890
I guess I could summarize
it where there's

00:15:39.890 --> 00:15:42.850
a lot of people in
the United States

00:15:42.850 --> 00:15:48.210
that the elite, on the
two coastal strips,

00:15:48.210 --> 00:15:50.790
say that they're
irredeemable or deplorable,

00:15:50.790 --> 00:15:53.430
or we say they're not knowledge
based or post-industrial.

00:15:53.430 --> 00:15:56.370
Whatever, they're
not in with it.

00:15:56.370 --> 00:15:58.140
They are very
angry at people who

00:15:58.140 --> 00:16:01.800
don't live by the ramifications
of their own ideology.

00:16:01.800 --> 00:16:05.370
And by that I mean,
if I'm in California,

00:16:05.370 --> 00:16:07.860
and I'm told we have to
have high speed rail,

00:16:07.860 --> 00:16:12.090
it's going to be built where
I live near Hanford and not

00:16:12.090 --> 00:16:14.340
in Palo Alto where
it should start.

00:16:14.340 --> 00:16:17.190
If I'm told we have to pay
the highest electricity

00:16:17.190 --> 00:16:21.270
rates in the country, it's
not going to be in Palo Alto

00:16:21.270 --> 00:16:22.470
where the temperature--

00:16:22.470 --> 00:16:24.150
I have an office at Stanford.

00:16:24.150 --> 00:16:27.210
I never turn on the
heat or the cooling.

00:16:27.210 --> 00:16:31.440
If I go in August 15th
when it's 107 in Fresno,

00:16:31.440 --> 00:16:33.600
Wal-Mart is full of Hispanic
people who are there

00:16:33.600 --> 00:16:34.810
for the air conditioning.

00:16:34.810 --> 00:16:38.910
They sit there all day, as if
their kids-- it's a playground.

00:16:38.910 --> 00:16:42.300
And so people, if I hear
a lecture from colleagues

00:16:42.300 --> 00:16:45.240
at Stanford and tell me that
open borders are a great thing,

00:16:45.240 --> 00:16:47.280
I know that they put
their kids in Castilleja,

00:16:47.280 --> 00:16:48.720
or Harker the Menlo school.

00:16:48.720 --> 00:16:51.390
They don't put them in
the Selma public schools.

00:16:51.390 --> 00:16:53.177
And there was a sense of anger.

00:16:53.177 --> 00:16:55.260
When I get up in the
morning, I don't get a letter

00:16:55.260 --> 00:16:58.260
and say Mr. Hanson, your column
from Tribune Media Services

00:16:58.260 --> 00:17:00.240
was outsourced to
somebody from South Korea.

00:17:00.240 --> 00:17:01.890
He can do it at half the price.

00:17:01.890 --> 00:17:04.020
It just doesn't occur to me.

00:17:04.020 --> 00:17:05.849
That occurs to a lot of people.

00:17:05.849 --> 00:17:11.460
And that was epitomized in
this Clinton couple that talked

00:17:11.460 --> 00:17:12.742
one way and lived the other.

00:17:12.742 --> 00:17:14.700
Progressive, progressive,
progressive, and then

00:17:14.700 --> 00:17:16.980
they basically had
a crime syndicate,

00:17:16.980 --> 00:17:19.530
and they had taste
and appetites that

00:17:19.530 --> 00:17:20.910
were entirely hypocritical.

00:17:20.910 --> 00:17:23.453
And you couldn't square the
circle of what they said

00:17:23.453 --> 00:17:25.619
and how they lived, and
that was apparent to people,

00:17:25.619 --> 00:17:29.820
that the Democratic Party had
become a party of the elite

00:17:29.820 --> 00:17:33.570
and a party of the
poor, and had not just

00:17:33.570 --> 00:17:37.410
uninterest in the middle,
but actual contempt for them.

00:17:37.410 --> 00:17:39.910
And that was fatal for
them in these states.

00:17:39.910 --> 00:17:41.910
I would say that you can
see that the Obamas are

00:17:41.910 --> 00:17:45.010
very much like the Clintons.

00:17:45.010 --> 00:17:49.180
I don't think Obama will be
the avatar of a movement.

00:17:49.180 --> 00:17:49.870
Think about it.

00:17:49.870 --> 00:17:51.490
Take those four people--

00:17:51.490 --> 00:17:53.830
Bill and Hillary,
Michelle and Barack.

00:17:53.830 --> 00:17:56.380
They're the only
post-presidential couples

00:17:56.380 --> 00:17:59.560
that went back, in our
era, to Washington.

00:17:59.560 --> 00:18:00.190
Carter didn't.

00:18:00.190 --> 00:18:02.140
Reagan and the Bushes didn't.

00:18:02.140 --> 00:18:03.640
They both bought mansions.

00:18:03.640 --> 00:18:05.710
They both organized
political action groups.

00:18:05.710 --> 00:18:07.630
They both, with a
wink and nod, said

00:18:07.630 --> 00:18:10.640
my wife may be still
politically active some day.

00:18:10.640 --> 00:18:15.370
Therefore, you should donate
to, check, the library, check,

00:18:15.370 --> 00:18:20.170
the foundation, check, the
$400,000 speaking engagements.

00:18:20.170 --> 00:18:22.840
They both looked at marriage
not maybe as an act of love

00:18:22.840 --> 00:18:25.450
so much as a
business proposition.

00:18:25.450 --> 00:18:27.910
They both had complaints
about how life had not

00:18:27.910 --> 00:18:30.850
treated them well and how they
deserved more money or more

00:18:30.850 --> 00:18:32.410
influence or power.

00:18:32.410 --> 00:18:34.240
And people looked
at Clinton, and I

00:18:34.240 --> 00:18:37.240
think the Obama, that
was really a bad image

00:18:37.240 --> 00:18:39.302
for the Democratic Party.

00:18:39.302 --> 00:18:41.260
People can take a lot of
things, but they don't

00:18:41.260 --> 00:18:42.730
like to be treated like idiots.

00:18:42.730 --> 00:18:45.580
And to the idea that these
are people in the trenches

00:18:45.580 --> 00:18:47.100
or they're good,
old boys or they

00:18:47.100 --> 00:18:48.910
are hardworking
progressives or you

00:18:48.910 --> 00:18:52.270
didn't build that, it's not time
to profit, or at some point you

00:18:52.270 --> 00:18:54.790
made enough money and
then go make $400,000.

00:18:54.790 --> 00:18:56.540
It just doesn't work.

00:18:56.540 --> 00:18:59.560
And so, I think that's
something to think about.

00:18:59.560 --> 00:19:01.960
As far as Trump himself--
so he's elected.

00:19:01.960 --> 00:19:04.270
And how are we going to
characterize the first 100

00:19:04.270 --> 00:19:06.100
days?

00:19:06.100 --> 00:19:08.410
Part of the problem
with the media hysteria

00:19:08.410 --> 00:19:11.110
is, again, if you've
lost the legislatures,

00:19:11.110 --> 00:19:13.720
the local offices,
governorships, Senate, House,

00:19:13.720 --> 00:19:16.290
then you don't have
political power.

00:19:16.290 --> 00:19:19.050
And so there's an anger at that.

00:19:19.050 --> 00:19:21.750
And if you're the party
of the very wealthy,

00:19:21.750 --> 00:19:24.960
the suburban, and the educated,
which now the Democratic Party

00:19:24.960 --> 00:19:28.530
is, if you look at the per
capita income of blue counties,

00:19:28.530 --> 00:19:31.420
overwhelmingly these
are Democratic.

00:19:31.420 --> 00:19:35.520
Then you're angry that
stupider people, less

00:19:35.520 --> 00:19:37.530
well-spoken people,
less educated,

00:19:37.530 --> 00:19:40.542
are dictating decisions that
you think affect your lives,

00:19:40.542 --> 00:19:42.750
and you don't know how to
respond because politically

00:19:42.750 --> 00:19:44.830
there's no outlet for it.

00:19:44.830 --> 00:19:48.880
And one of the ironies is the
media now is the other party.

00:19:48.880 --> 00:19:52.230
So if the media starts to use
the F-word or the shit word

00:19:52.230 --> 00:19:54.660
or whatever that, then
politicians follow suit.

00:19:54.660 --> 00:19:56.160
Not vice versa.

00:19:56.160 --> 00:19:59.520
If the media builds up Black
Lives Matter or transgender

00:19:59.520 --> 00:20:04.590
Hollywood celebrities create
LGBT movement or global warming

00:20:04.590 --> 00:20:06.780
comes out of the culture,
then the Democratic Party

00:20:06.780 --> 00:20:08.200
reacts to it.

00:20:08.200 --> 00:20:12.840
So this hysteria from Trump
is, in the democratic sense,

00:20:12.840 --> 00:20:13.440
reactive.

00:20:13.440 --> 00:20:15.840
Chuck Schumer reacts
to Trump after what he

00:20:15.840 --> 00:20:18.600
reads in the media about Trump.

00:20:18.600 --> 00:20:20.730
And they don't really
have a political base

00:20:20.730 --> 00:20:25.290
because Dianne Feinstein
is 83, Nancy Pelosi

00:20:25.290 --> 00:20:28.980
is 79, Steny Hoyer,
77, Jerry Brown is 80,

00:20:28.980 --> 00:20:31.157
Bernie Sanders is 76.

00:20:31.157 --> 00:20:33.240
And the people who are
young are more frightening.

00:20:33.240 --> 00:20:35.520
I mean Keith Ellison and Perez.

00:20:35.520 --> 00:20:39.362
So they don't have a second
team that's going to come in.

00:20:39.362 --> 00:20:41.070
Whatever you think
about the Republicans,

00:20:41.070 --> 00:20:44.460
they've got young people
like Paul Ryan in the House,

00:20:44.460 --> 00:20:46.460
and they've got young
governors, Marco Rubio.

00:20:46.460 --> 00:20:48.836
So there's a frustration
that, politically,

00:20:48.836 --> 00:20:50.460
they don't know what
to do, and they're

00:20:50.460 --> 00:20:54.090
angry at this guy who doesn't
fit their image of a president.

00:20:54.090 --> 00:20:55.150
He doesn't speak well.

00:20:55.150 --> 00:20:58.239
He's not educated in the
way that they think he is.

00:20:58.239 --> 00:21:00.780
And their way of thinking-- and
I've tried this conversation,

00:21:00.780 --> 00:21:04.590
maybe some of you have with left
wing people who hate Trump--

00:21:04.590 --> 00:21:07.050
I said, you think it's easy to
make a buck in the Manhattan

00:21:07.050 --> 00:21:08.490
real estate world?

00:21:08.490 --> 00:21:11.070
Crooked unions, crooked
politicians, crooked community

00:21:11.070 --> 00:21:13.500
organizing, crooked
environmentalists, and you're

00:21:13.500 --> 00:21:15.859
going to build a skyscraper
in downtown Manhattan.

00:21:15.859 --> 00:21:17.400
You've got to be a
genius to survive.

00:21:17.400 --> 00:21:19.080
They don't see that.

00:21:19.080 --> 00:21:21.090
So they underestimated
him in the campaign,

00:21:21.090 --> 00:21:23.490
and now as president, they
are underestimating him.

00:21:23.490 --> 00:21:26.900
So what is he trying to do?

00:21:26.900 --> 00:21:29.090
Basically, if you look at
the agenda, what he's done

00:21:29.090 --> 00:21:34.360
is a 1995 to 2005 agenda.

00:21:34.360 --> 00:21:36.290
The proposed tax
reforms are more

00:21:36.290 --> 00:21:40.560
like George Bush's
reduction from 39 to 35

00:21:40.560 --> 00:21:43.250
than they are Ronald
Reagan's down to 28.

00:21:43.250 --> 00:21:46.310
If you look at Keystone
and Dakota pipelines,

00:21:46.310 --> 00:21:49.130
they were already approved.

00:21:49.130 --> 00:21:50.780
It was pretty a
mainstream decision.

00:21:50.780 --> 00:21:54.600
Hillary was all for
Keystone until the election.

00:21:54.600 --> 00:21:56.960
If you look at
immigration, Trump

00:21:56.960 --> 00:21:59.480
didn't come up with
the idea of the wall.

00:21:59.480 --> 00:22:01.070
The House Republicans had voted.

00:22:01.070 --> 00:22:03.050
The whole Senate approved it.

00:22:03.050 --> 00:22:06.230
A third of it had been
built. Listen sometimes

00:22:06.230 --> 00:22:08.480
to Bill Clinton and
one of his speeches

00:22:08.480 --> 00:22:11.000
from 1994 on
illegal immigration.

00:22:11.000 --> 00:22:15.500
It's far to the right of Donald
Trump, and so is Harry Reid's.

00:22:15.500 --> 00:22:19.100
The point that I'm getting
at is that Trump went back

00:22:19.100 --> 00:22:21.050
to the middle, but
the middle no longer

00:22:21.050 --> 00:22:22.820
existed as we had known it.

00:22:22.820 --> 00:22:25.250
So it was considered
radical and revolutionary

00:22:25.250 --> 00:22:28.130
because they were convinced
that this trajectory, whether it

00:22:28.130 --> 00:22:32.720
was a single payer or permanent
identity politics or whatever

00:22:32.720 --> 00:22:36.830
or lead from behind foreign
policy, was set in stone.

00:22:36.830 --> 00:22:41.270
And trying to bring
everybody back was difficult.

00:22:41.270 --> 00:22:43.310
The other thing that
I think frightened

00:22:43.310 --> 00:22:46.550
them is that they were afraid
that-- and we're starting

00:22:46.550 --> 00:22:48.590
to see it in Trump's
first 100 days--

00:22:48.590 --> 00:22:51.290
is that he's not as
stupid as we think.

00:22:51.290 --> 00:22:55.640
There is a political
paradigm behind a lot

00:22:55.640 --> 00:22:57.560
of his economic policies.

00:22:57.560 --> 00:23:02.770
And I think it's summed
up by two words--

00:23:02.770 --> 00:23:10.000
3%, Joe Biden said it was
three, maybe four, 3% growth.

00:23:10.000 --> 00:23:14.080
If he gets 3% growth due to
simplifying the tax code,

00:23:14.080 --> 00:23:17.760
reducing corporate
taxes, deregulation

00:23:17.760 --> 00:23:19.240
in some key
industries, especially

00:23:19.240 --> 00:23:22.510
EPA, getting the interest
rates maybe up to 1%

00:23:22.510 --> 00:23:25.310
or 2% or 3% higher
than they are now.

00:23:25.310 --> 00:23:27.830
And he can achieve 3%
growth that Obama never did,

00:23:27.830 --> 00:23:29.610
and we haven't had in 10 years.

00:23:29.610 --> 00:23:31.930
Then Trump's idea is
that whatever differences

00:23:31.930 --> 00:23:37.060
you have with one another on
your religion or race or skin

00:23:37.060 --> 00:23:40.210
color or ethnic background or
whatever animosity that you

00:23:40.210 --> 00:23:43.180
have to me, it's
going to disappear.

00:23:43.180 --> 00:23:46.030
And as somebody who lived
through Reagan's era,

00:23:46.030 --> 00:23:49.550
I can tell you that people
hated Ronald Reagan.

00:23:49.550 --> 00:23:52.120
And he was very
unpopular in 81 and 82.

00:23:52.120 --> 00:23:57.400
I remember there was a
congressman named Reagan,

00:23:57.400 --> 00:23:59.680
and his last name was Vineyard.

00:23:59.680 --> 00:24:01.000
And he ran for office.

00:24:01.000 --> 00:24:02.770
Reagan Vineyard.

00:24:02.770 --> 00:24:03.956
And somebody went down to--

00:24:03.956 --> 00:24:05.830
I think it was in Southern
California-- found

00:24:05.830 --> 00:24:10.480
this obscure, failed candidate
and made his campaign

00:24:10.480 --> 00:24:12.020
and printed up
thousands of copies.

00:24:12.020 --> 00:24:14.110
And all over where
we were, because we

00:24:14.110 --> 00:24:17.770
had an agricultural collapse
when Paul Volcker sort of broke

00:24:17.770 --> 00:24:20.890
inflation, prices just crashed.

00:24:20.890 --> 00:24:23.550
And everybody had this sign
in their- Thompson seedless

00:24:23.550 --> 00:24:25.300
raisin, Reagan Vineyard.

00:24:25.300 --> 00:24:25.810
We had one.

00:24:25.810 --> 00:24:27.880
Reagan Vineyard,
Reagan Vineyard.

00:24:27.880 --> 00:24:30.250
And everybody said
this guy cannot win.

00:24:30.250 --> 00:24:32.320
And then you turn
on the TV, and it

00:24:32.320 --> 00:24:34.780
was the day after
the nuclear winter I

00:24:34.780 --> 00:24:37.060
think Carl Sagan told us about.

00:24:37.060 --> 00:24:39.160
Deploying Pershing
missiles in Germany

00:24:39.160 --> 00:24:41.410
was going to start
a nuclear war.

00:24:41.410 --> 00:24:44.140
He was bombing,
invading Grenada.

00:24:44.140 --> 00:24:45.370
He was just Trumpian.

00:24:48.580 --> 00:24:51.250
And Walter Mondale was
out of central casting,

00:24:51.250 --> 00:24:56.950
tall, handsome, liberal, but
not crazy liberal like McGovern.

00:24:56.950 --> 00:24:58.690
And he was going to win.

00:24:58.690 --> 00:25:01.930
And suddenly between, I
think the dates are roughly

00:25:01.930 --> 00:25:07.420
between 1983 in November to
October 1984, the economy

00:25:07.420 --> 00:25:10.540
grew at a blistering 7.1%.

00:25:10.540 --> 00:25:11.950
And suddenly it didn't matter.

00:25:11.950 --> 00:25:13.450
Reagan could have
blown up the world

00:25:13.450 --> 00:25:15.892
and people would've said
at least I died rich.

00:25:15.892 --> 00:25:20.110
So, people switched.

00:25:20.110 --> 00:25:22.450
And it was miraculous
for me to see

00:25:22.450 --> 00:25:26.890
all these farmers who hated him,
and all of a sudden they said--

00:25:26.890 --> 00:25:28.540
one guy, I can
remember, did this.

00:25:28.540 --> 00:25:30.940
He took pruning
shears and sheared off

00:25:30.940 --> 00:25:33.680
the vineyard sign in his
barn and brought it back out,

00:25:33.680 --> 00:25:36.240
and it said Reagan with this
ragged edge on the bottom.

00:25:36.240 --> 00:25:39.250
So he was no longer Reagan
Vineyard, but it was Reagan.

00:25:39.250 --> 00:25:41.380
And that's what I think
has influenced Trump.

00:25:41.380 --> 00:25:45.460
He feels that, as a business
person, that money talks.

00:25:45.460 --> 00:25:47.170
And if we're prosperous--

00:25:47.170 --> 00:25:50.590
and so in this way,
although we're told that

00:25:50.590 --> 00:25:54.400
he's a protectionist, or
that he's a mercantilist,

00:25:54.400 --> 00:25:56.050
or he's pre-Milton--

00:25:56.050 --> 00:26:01.540
whatever that is, if you look
at his actual economic team,

00:26:01.540 --> 00:26:05.690
their idea is to
grow the economy.

00:26:05.690 --> 00:26:08.140
And I think that explains a
lot of what he's doing along

00:26:08.140 --> 00:26:12.100
with bringing people back to
the center on cultural issues.

00:26:12.100 --> 00:26:14.595
Abroad, it's pretty simple.

00:26:17.650 --> 00:26:22.110
If we were to take a survey
of the world on January 20,

00:26:22.110 --> 00:26:26.550
I think all of us would say
that North Korea that Obama

00:26:26.550 --> 00:26:28.440
bequeathed is not
a stable situation.

00:26:28.440 --> 00:26:29.520
They have been testing.

00:26:29.520 --> 00:26:31.770
They had been threatening
to blow up people.

00:26:31.770 --> 00:26:34.410
And they had been doing
this predicated on the idea

00:26:34.410 --> 00:26:37.380
that being nuts and having
a nuke or two was a good way

00:26:37.380 --> 00:26:39.000
to extort money.

00:26:39.000 --> 00:26:40.620
And it would continue.

00:26:40.620 --> 00:26:42.600
If you looked at
Iran, we were starting

00:26:42.600 --> 00:26:45.600
to discover there was a
side deal on inspections.

00:26:45.600 --> 00:26:47.820
There was a side
deal on hostages.

00:26:47.820 --> 00:26:50.190
Cash for hostages,
we didn't know about.

00:26:50.190 --> 00:26:53.590
There was a side deal for
ballistic missile development.

00:26:53.590 --> 00:26:56.850
And again, the premise was
that Obama would or could not

00:26:56.850 --> 00:26:59.430
do anything about it,
and the United States

00:26:59.430 --> 00:27:01.200
was a played out power.

00:27:01.200 --> 00:27:03.720
If we looked at Russia,
we were not talking just

00:27:03.720 --> 00:27:04.920
about Crimea, Ukraine.

00:27:04.920 --> 00:27:06.810
We were talking about
the next acquisition

00:27:06.810 --> 00:27:08.730
in the Baltic states.

00:27:08.730 --> 00:27:13.180
We'd agreed that
reset had failed,

00:27:13.180 --> 00:27:16.090
dismantling the missile
project, anti-ballistic missile

00:27:16.090 --> 00:27:19.000
in Eastern Europe, or
making fun of Mitt Romney

00:27:19.000 --> 00:27:22.870
to assure Putin would be more
flexible after the election.

00:27:22.870 --> 00:27:25.840
All of that stuff had failed.

00:27:25.840 --> 00:27:27.160
And Russia was emboldened.

00:27:27.160 --> 00:27:30.800
China had built these bases
on the Spratly Islands,

00:27:30.800 --> 00:27:32.350
was on the move.

00:27:32.350 --> 00:27:33.610
It all reminded me--

00:27:33.610 --> 00:27:36.580
again, I don't want to overdo
this imagery-- but remember,

00:27:36.580 --> 00:27:38.800
Jimmy Carter ran
on not one soldier

00:27:38.800 --> 00:27:42.040
shall die under my tenure.

00:27:42.040 --> 00:27:46.960
I have no inordinate fear of
communism like my opponent,

00:27:46.960 --> 00:27:50.380
and human rights shall
adjudicate my foreign policy

00:27:50.380 --> 00:27:51.670
positions.

00:27:51.670 --> 00:27:53.200
Remember all that?

00:27:53.200 --> 00:27:58.551
And then I remember he wanted
to sell F-15s to Saudi Arabia

00:27:58.551 --> 00:28:00.550
and the compromise is he
would get to sell them,

00:28:00.550 --> 00:28:03.010
but they wouldn't have
any bomb racks on them.

00:28:03.010 --> 00:28:04.960
So we sold them these
things, but they

00:28:04.960 --> 00:28:08.380
had to sort of jerry-rigg
the bomb racks on them.

00:28:08.380 --> 00:28:12.460
So and then we found out
that after three years,

00:28:12.460 --> 00:28:14.200
his therapeutic
view of human nature

00:28:14.200 --> 00:28:19.120
ended up with China invading
Vietnam, communist uprisings

00:28:19.120 --> 00:28:22.060
in Central America, the
Russians in Afghanistan,

00:28:22.060 --> 00:28:23.920
a revolution in
Iran, the Shah gone,

00:28:23.920 --> 00:28:26.770
and the Shia radical Islam.

00:28:26.770 --> 00:28:28.270
And then Reagan
came in and remember

00:28:28.270 --> 00:28:29.560
he didn't really say anything.

00:28:29.560 --> 00:28:31.420
There will be no hostages.

00:28:31.420 --> 00:28:32.650
What did that mean?

00:28:32.650 --> 00:28:34.570
And that point was
he was unpredictable,

00:28:34.570 --> 00:28:36.700
and he would restore deterrence.

00:28:36.700 --> 00:28:39.820
And then when he sent missiles
into Germany, stationed

00:28:39.820 --> 00:28:43.430
them there in Libya,
Grenada, as I said earlier,

00:28:43.430 --> 00:28:45.480
the loss of deterrence
is very dangerous,

00:28:45.480 --> 00:28:48.490
and it's matched only by
the effort to recapture it.

00:28:48.490 --> 00:28:51.370
So what Trump is trying
to do I think via Mattis

00:28:51.370 --> 00:28:54.510
and McMaster and that team--

00:28:54.510 --> 00:28:57.010
it's not neo con nation
building obviously,

00:28:57.010 --> 00:29:00.700
it's not isolationism,
it's not even just realism.

00:29:00.700 --> 00:29:05.020
It's sort of strategic
deterrence or realist

00:29:05.020 --> 00:29:05.620
deterrence.

00:29:05.620 --> 00:29:07.720
And what they're saying
is that the world

00:29:07.720 --> 00:29:12.670
is an unstable, dangerous
place because people

00:29:12.670 --> 00:29:19.122
have come to incorrect
conjectures about US power.

00:29:19.122 --> 00:29:21.580
And that's a very dangerous
time in world history, whenever

00:29:21.580 --> 00:29:22.360
that happens.

00:29:22.360 --> 00:29:24.880
In other words, by any
empirical standard,

00:29:24.880 --> 00:29:27.730
the United States
military, as cut back

00:29:27.730 --> 00:29:31.720
as it has been under Obama where
we're down to below almost 3%

00:29:31.720 --> 00:29:34.540
GDP investment, if you
look at our carrier

00:29:34.540 --> 00:29:38.830
force, our strategic
assets, our nuclear assets,

00:29:38.830 --> 00:29:41.410
our battle-hardened
troops fighting in places

00:29:41.410 --> 00:29:43.690
like Afghanistan,
there's no military

00:29:43.690 --> 00:29:46.090
that even approximates--
there's no two militaries

00:29:46.090 --> 00:29:47.650
together that approximate.

00:29:47.650 --> 00:29:51.250
But deterrence is
also a frame of mind.

00:29:51.250 --> 00:29:54.890
But if you believe that that
military will never be used

00:29:54.890 --> 00:29:57.040
or it will be predictably
used, then you

00:29:57.040 --> 00:29:59.560
earn the additional
wage of contempt.

00:29:59.560 --> 00:30:03.310
As Hitler said, there's
no greater contempt

00:30:03.310 --> 00:30:07.990
for a military superior power
that you can push around.

00:30:07.990 --> 00:30:11.200
And people kept telling Hitler,
don't go into the Saarland.

00:30:11.200 --> 00:30:13.180
Don't screw around the
Saarland elections.

00:30:13.180 --> 00:30:14.620
Don't go in the Rhineland.

00:30:14.620 --> 00:30:16.720
The French army could crush us.

00:30:16.720 --> 00:30:24.509
And don't go into Poland because
Britain and France together

00:30:24.509 --> 00:30:25.300
have a bigger army.

00:30:25.300 --> 00:30:26.260
They have better tanks.

00:30:26.260 --> 00:30:30.250
A Char B tank is so much
superior to Mark I, II, or III.

00:30:30.250 --> 00:30:35.290
The new Spitfire has the same
characteristics as the MBF109.

00:30:35.290 --> 00:30:36.130
Don't do this.

00:30:36.130 --> 00:30:39.940
And he said I saw
Chamberlain at Munich.

00:30:39.940 --> 00:30:41.022
I wanted to step on him.

00:30:41.022 --> 00:30:43.480
I wanted to stomp his stomach
and take that stupid umbrella

00:30:43.480 --> 00:30:45.244
and hit him over
the head with it.

00:30:45.244 --> 00:30:46.660
And they're worms,
he called them.

00:30:46.660 --> 00:30:49.660
People who had been
magnanimous were called worms.

00:30:49.660 --> 00:30:51.430
And so we lost deterrence.

00:30:51.430 --> 00:30:52.460
And so what are wars?

00:30:52.460 --> 00:30:55.420
Wars are a laboratory experiment
to prove who was actually

00:30:55.420 --> 00:30:56.980
strong in the first place.

00:30:56.980 --> 00:30:59.440
Because once you have
faulty perceptions,

00:30:59.440 --> 00:31:01.000
reality is often the check.

00:31:01.000 --> 00:31:04.990
As I said earlier today,
if you're in high school--

00:31:04.990 --> 00:31:07.030
I went to a really
rough high school--

00:31:07.030 --> 00:31:09.820
I remember a guy named
Armando Quintana.

00:31:09.820 --> 00:31:13.166
And he'd always
come on like this.

00:31:13.166 --> 00:31:14.290
I'm going to get you after.

00:31:14.290 --> 00:31:15.490
And he'd do this.

00:31:15.490 --> 00:31:19.229
And he was about 5' 1".

00:31:19.229 --> 00:31:21.520
And there was a guy named
Geronimo Rodriguez who was 6'

00:31:21.520 --> 00:31:22.020
3".

00:31:22.020 --> 00:31:25.615
He was from Mexico illegally,
and they put everybody- fights

00:31:25.615 --> 00:31:26.740
until they learned English.

00:31:26.740 --> 00:31:29.080
And Geronimo was the
nicest guy in the world.

00:31:29.080 --> 00:31:32.590
He just sat like this and
then, go up and hit him.

00:31:32.590 --> 00:31:34.000
I'm going to take you out.

00:31:34.000 --> 00:31:36.190
And one day, Geronimo
just flattened him.

00:31:36.190 --> 00:31:38.170
I mean, he knocked
him five feet.

00:31:38.170 --> 00:31:39.970
Didn't say a word,
just knocked him.

00:31:39.970 --> 00:31:40.730
Broke his jaw.

00:31:40.730 --> 00:31:43.320
It was a big hubbub
in those days.

00:31:43.320 --> 00:31:44.320
They dismissed school.

00:31:44.320 --> 00:31:46.090
Everybody could not believe it.

00:31:46.090 --> 00:31:47.620
And I thought,
wow, that's weird.

00:31:47.620 --> 00:31:49.420
And I think what
Geronimo was trying

00:31:49.420 --> 00:31:51.104
to do was restore deterrence.

00:31:51.104 --> 00:31:53.020
But he had to do it in
a very violent way that

00:31:53.020 --> 00:31:55.020
was entirely unnecessary.

00:31:55.020 --> 00:31:58.480
Had he just said, Mr. Quintana,
I would not do that again.

00:31:58.480 --> 00:32:00.370
Or just tapped
him once, he would

00:32:00.370 --> 00:32:03.250
have established the rules.

00:32:03.250 --> 00:32:06.550
When Dean Acheson said
that South Korea is not

00:32:06.550 --> 00:32:10.700
within our realm of
defensive capable assistance,

00:32:10.700 --> 00:32:13.740
the north invaded just
three months later.

00:32:13.740 --> 00:32:16.310
When April Gillespie
said we don't

00:32:16.310 --> 00:32:20.000
intervene with borders
between Kuwait and Iraq,

00:32:20.000 --> 00:32:22.160
and then Saddam invaded.

00:32:22.160 --> 00:32:24.840
And we forget that,
that gestures,

00:32:24.840 --> 00:32:29.330
statements, assurances to
unpredictable aggressors

00:32:29.330 --> 00:32:32.450
that we won't react are
very, very dangerous.

00:32:32.450 --> 00:32:37.890
I had a member of my family
said if Trump is elected,

00:32:37.890 --> 00:32:40.270
you'll be so scared the
world is going to blow up.

00:32:40.270 --> 00:32:41.769
I said no, it will
be the first time

00:32:41.769 --> 00:32:44.400
I rest in peace because I've
been terrified the last eight

00:32:44.400 --> 00:32:47.280
years that some weaker
power would do something

00:32:47.280 --> 00:32:50.640
stupid on the premise that
the United States would not

00:32:50.640 --> 00:32:51.450
retaliate.

00:32:51.450 --> 00:32:53.850
And then we would get in
a war, which would finally

00:32:53.850 --> 00:32:56.910
prove after many deaths
and much violence, that

00:32:56.910 --> 00:32:59.610
guess what, the United States
really is stronger than Iran.

00:33:02.910 --> 00:33:05.880
One carrier group has more
conventional and nuclear power,

00:33:05.880 --> 00:33:09.820
of course nuclear, than the
entire Iranian military.

00:33:09.820 --> 00:33:12.330
It would be stupid for
Iran to hijack a boat

00:33:12.330 --> 00:33:14.790
or humiliate its crew or
send a missile near a carrier

00:33:14.790 --> 00:33:17.100
and yet, that was
what they were doing.

00:33:17.100 --> 00:33:20.340
Because they felt that they had
contempt for military stronger

00:33:20.340 --> 00:33:22.470
power that would never use it.

00:33:22.470 --> 00:33:24.300
And then you get
Trump in, and one

00:33:24.300 --> 00:33:28.230
of the things about restoring
deterrence is he's capable,

00:33:28.230 --> 00:33:29.700
he thinks, or he
tries to project

00:33:29.700 --> 00:33:31.740
that image of saying
anything to anyone any day

00:33:31.740 --> 00:33:33.360
and doing anything.

00:33:33.360 --> 00:33:35.160
I was watching this
strange media reaction

00:33:35.160 --> 00:33:36.840
to the firing of Comey.

00:33:36.840 --> 00:33:40.710
One of my colleagues at Hoover
wrote today, this is terrible.

00:33:40.710 --> 00:33:41.670
He fired Comey.

00:33:41.670 --> 00:33:44.590
And that's going to
unsettle everybody overseas.

00:33:44.590 --> 00:33:46.550
And I thought, no, it's not.

00:33:46.550 --> 00:33:48.840
It's going to be like Reagan
firing the air traffic

00:33:48.840 --> 00:33:50.927
controllers when Gorbachev
said don't screw around

00:33:50.927 --> 00:33:51.510
with this guy.

00:33:51.510 --> 00:33:53.430
He's capable of anything.

00:33:53.430 --> 00:33:54.930
So I don't think
people understand

00:33:54.930 --> 00:33:58.060
because they don't live
outside of that coastal bubble.

00:33:58.060 --> 00:34:00.690
That the world operates on a
tragic view of human nature

00:34:00.690 --> 00:34:02.730
rather than this
therapeutic view.

00:34:02.730 --> 00:34:06.270
And the therapeutic
view is very dangerous.

00:34:06.270 --> 00:34:11.130
And the first Lord Halifax,
I think it was in 1785,

00:34:11.130 --> 00:34:13.710
the great jurist of
British common law

00:34:13.710 --> 00:34:16.469
said when people were
trying to oppose the death

00:34:16.469 --> 00:34:18.989
penalty for anything
other than capital crimes,

00:34:18.989 --> 00:34:22.300
he said well, you know,
we hang horse thieves,

00:34:22.300 --> 00:34:24.469
not because the
guy stole a horse,

00:34:24.469 --> 00:34:27.060
but because we don't
want horses to be stolen.

00:34:27.060 --> 00:34:28.800
And that's what deterrence is.

00:34:28.800 --> 00:34:34.230
We don't like to do a crazy
MOAB bomb or bomb one airline.

00:34:34.230 --> 00:34:35.620
But that's not
the purpose of it.

00:34:35.620 --> 00:34:38.223
It's to remind people with
subterranean capabilities

00:34:38.223 --> 00:34:39.889
that you shouldn't
do that because we're

00:34:39.889 --> 00:34:43.590
capable of doing something,
like not announcing it.

00:34:43.590 --> 00:34:46.350
And Trump would probably say,
if I have an FBI director,

00:34:46.350 --> 00:34:49.350
it would be useful for him
to know that I can wake up

00:34:49.350 --> 00:34:51.480
one morning and fire him.

00:34:51.480 --> 00:34:53.230
Because that creates
a deterrent attitude,

00:34:53.230 --> 00:34:55.050
and you don't have that
complacency or even

00:34:55.050 --> 00:34:58.170
arrogance and hubris that James
Comey did because he understood

00:34:58.170 --> 00:35:00.090
that nobody would
ever question them.

00:35:00.090 --> 00:35:02.880
So, a lot of the
misinterpretation

00:35:02.880 --> 00:35:06.070
of what Trump is doing overseas,
I think can be summed up,

00:35:06.070 --> 00:35:10.560
is it's very dangerous to
try to restore deterrence.

00:35:10.560 --> 00:35:14.730
And I don't know if it's a
thought out Trump doctrine,

00:35:14.730 --> 00:35:17.670
but I do, as someone who was
a colleague of Jim Mattis

00:35:17.670 --> 00:35:21.630
at Hoover and knows HR
a little bit, McMaster.

00:35:21.630 --> 00:35:24.360
I think they are
trying to tell Trump

00:35:24.360 --> 00:35:27.750
that we have to find symbolic
iconic moments that will not

00:35:27.750 --> 00:35:29.110
lead to a wider war.

00:35:29.110 --> 00:35:31.530
And then we have to draw
a line, and a real line,

00:35:31.530 --> 00:35:33.870
not verbally but in actuality.

00:35:33.870 --> 00:35:35.750
And after a few of
these incidents,

00:35:35.750 --> 00:35:37.500
in which we're going
to take a lot of heat

00:35:37.500 --> 00:35:39.640
and which are going
to be very dangerous,

00:35:39.640 --> 00:35:41.490
we will reestablish
deterrence and then

00:35:41.490 --> 00:35:44.460
the world as we knew it in the
post-war order will return,

00:35:44.460 --> 00:35:47.130
and this is preferable
to the alternative.

00:35:47.130 --> 00:35:49.050
If you would ask
what Trump would say,

00:35:49.050 --> 00:35:52.332
I would probably say something--

00:35:52.332 --> 00:35:53.790
I don't believe in
nation building.

00:35:53.790 --> 00:35:56.130
I don't think we can
change the nature of man.

00:35:56.130 --> 00:35:58.160
The world abroad is always
going to have thugs.

00:35:58.160 --> 00:35:59.090
It's like your lawn.

00:35:59.090 --> 00:36:00.220
It's always going to grow.

00:36:00.220 --> 00:36:02.940
And the idea that you're going
to wave a wand, and the grass

00:36:02.940 --> 00:36:03.800
will be perfect.

00:36:03.800 --> 00:36:05.790
You'll have to buy
AstroTurf in fantasy land

00:36:05.790 --> 00:36:07.950
because you have
to mow the lawn.

00:36:07.950 --> 00:36:09.979
And there's always going
to be a Saddam Hussein.

00:36:09.979 --> 00:36:11.520
There's always going
to be a Noriega.

00:36:11.520 --> 00:36:13.145
There's always going
to be a Milosevic.

00:36:13.145 --> 00:36:15.420
There's always going
to be a Mullah Omar.

00:36:15.420 --> 00:36:19.650
And the degree to which they
can harm innocent people

00:36:19.650 --> 00:36:22.350
depends on how well
they are deterred.

00:36:22.350 --> 00:36:23.850
And we've got to
restore deterrence,

00:36:23.850 --> 00:36:25.920
and this is the way to do it.

00:36:25.920 --> 00:36:28.410
Where does all this
lead in conclusion,

00:36:28.410 --> 00:36:34.120
as far as the 2020 election?

00:36:34.120 --> 00:36:38.110
Well, Donald Trump has
some inconsistencies

00:36:38.110 --> 00:36:40.390
that he ran on certain
principles that

00:36:40.390 --> 00:36:43.210
got these proverbial
Reagan Democrats,

00:36:43.210 --> 00:36:45.520
or the white working
class, or the forgotten

00:36:45.520 --> 00:36:48.220
Americans, or the
interior Americans,

00:36:48.220 --> 00:36:51.490
or the blue wall Americans
out to vote in a way

00:36:51.490 --> 00:36:53.110
that they didn't before.

00:36:53.110 --> 00:36:55.960
I was teaching at
Hillsdale this year,

00:36:55.960 --> 00:36:59.860
and I had taught there in the
2004, '12, and '16 election.

00:36:59.860 --> 00:37:02.470
I had never seen signs
like I saw before.

00:37:02.470 --> 00:37:05.720
When I came back to California,
a Hoover colleague said to me,

00:37:05.720 --> 00:37:07.480
well, my latest poll--
he's a pollster--

00:37:07.480 --> 00:37:10.090
said that Trump is
going to lose by 11.

00:37:10.090 --> 00:37:12.400
And I said, I think he's
going to win Michigan.

00:37:12.400 --> 00:37:14.710
And they said, well that's
because you rely on anecdote

00:37:14.710 --> 00:37:16.600
and you watch TV,
and I have analytics.

00:37:16.600 --> 00:37:21.280
I said no, it's because I rode
a bike for almost 25 days,

00:37:21.280 --> 00:37:24.280
and I saw posters everywhere.

00:37:24.280 --> 00:37:28.090
And at one point, I saw a person
spray painting his garage.

00:37:28.090 --> 00:37:29.351
And I thought he was a vandal.

00:37:29.351 --> 00:37:30.850
So I got off my
bike, and I ran over

00:37:30.850 --> 00:37:34.510
and I said, you just spray
painted make America great.

00:37:34.510 --> 00:37:35.049
Who are you?

00:37:35.049 --> 00:37:36.340
Did you know the owner of this?

00:37:36.340 --> 00:37:39.294
He said, I am the owner.

00:37:39.294 --> 00:37:40.960
This is the only time
I'm going to vote.

00:37:40.960 --> 00:37:41.950
I don't vote anymore.

00:37:41.950 --> 00:37:43.510
I'm going to vote for
this guy, and everybody

00:37:43.510 --> 00:37:45.593
you see around here is
going to vote for this guy.

00:37:45.593 --> 00:37:47.290
And I thought,
wow, if that would

00:37:47.290 --> 00:37:51.100
be replicated throughout the
blue wall states, he could win.

00:37:51.100 --> 00:37:52.900
And then when we
got home, I said

00:37:52.900 --> 00:37:56.740
to my wife, who teaches junior
college, I think he can win.

00:37:56.740 --> 00:37:59.330
He said, well, you
know, it's very funny.

00:37:59.330 --> 00:38:01.010
I have mostly Hispanic class.

00:38:01.010 --> 00:38:02.780
I was making a
joke, and I said I

00:38:02.780 --> 00:38:05.000
guess nobody will
be voting for Trump,

00:38:05.000 --> 00:38:08.890
and the girl in the front
said why don't you poll.

00:38:08.890 --> 00:38:10.790
26 to 23.

00:38:10.790 --> 00:38:13.910
Trump had 23 votes of
all Hispanic students

00:38:13.910 --> 00:38:15.870
in the interior of California.

00:38:15.870 --> 00:38:17.280
These things started popping up.

00:38:17.280 --> 00:38:18.571
I know it happened to you, too.

00:38:18.571 --> 00:38:20.480
All the time, we were
told it was impossible.

00:38:21.170 --> 00:38:24.060
So, Trump appealed to
working class people who

00:38:24.060 --> 00:38:27.030
were sick and tired of
this elite or the media,

00:38:27.030 --> 00:38:29.270
whoever we call the elite.

00:38:29.270 --> 00:38:33.260
And that base is all
that matters to him.

00:38:33.260 --> 00:38:36.950
We know now that conservative
pundits that do what I do

00:38:36.950 --> 00:38:39.230
have no influence whatsoever.

00:38:39.230 --> 00:38:40.640
If George Will writes a column--

00:38:40.640 --> 00:38:42.681
I like him a great deal,
I have respect for him--

00:38:42.681 --> 00:38:44.450
but if he writes a
column saying Trump

00:38:44.450 --> 00:38:48.530
is the arch satanic figure of
our time, nobody in Bakersfield

00:38:48.530 --> 00:38:50.270
is going to change his vote.

00:38:50.270 --> 00:38:51.950
Just not going to happen.

00:38:51.950 --> 00:38:54.600
My magazine that I
write for, The National,

00:38:54.600 --> 00:38:57.452
if they run an entire
issue saying never Trump,

00:38:57.452 --> 00:38:58.910
the people of
Michigan don't get up

00:38:58.910 --> 00:39:02.400
in the morning saying I
just can't vote for him.

00:39:02.400 --> 00:39:06.150
It's just not going to happen.

00:39:06.150 --> 00:39:08.630
But what is going to
happen is those states

00:39:08.630 --> 00:39:10.110
have to be motivated.

00:39:10.110 --> 00:39:12.320
And they voted for him
for four or five issues,

00:39:12.320 --> 00:39:13.445
and you know what they are.

00:39:13.445 --> 00:39:15.480
One of them was
illegal immigration.

00:39:15.480 --> 00:39:16.980
Trump was the
first candidate who

00:39:16.980 --> 00:39:19.524
said I don't have
to do amnesty lite.

00:39:19.524 --> 00:39:20.940
I don't have to
the Gang of Eight.

00:39:20.940 --> 00:39:23.023
I don't have to do Marco
Rubio's- The law says you

00:39:23.023 --> 00:39:25.140
can't come in if you're illegal.

00:39:25.140 --> 00:39:26.610
I'm not going to
deport everybody.

00:39:26.610 --> 00:39:28.290
If you're here, you haven't
had a criminal record,

00:39:28.290 --> 00:39:30.748
you've been here for five years,
you're working, maybe, OK.

00:39:30.748 --> 00:39:33.020
But we got some bad hombres.

00:39:33.020 --> 00:39:35.190
And that was true, we do.

00:39:35.190 --> 00:39:39.230
And he basically said Mexico
benefits from remittances.

00:39:39.230 --> 00:39:42.300
They export the poor rather
than addressing poverty.

00:39:42.300 --> 00:39:43.950
It's a safety valve for them.

00:39:43.950 --> 00:39:47.010
La Raza wants to change the
demographics of the American

00:39:47.010 --> 00:39:47.670
Southwest.

00:39:47.670 --> 00:39:50.640
The Democratic Party
wants to usher people in.

00:39:50.640 --> 00:39:52.080
They do not want
the melting pot.

00:39:52.080 --> 00:39:53.760
They do not want
Italian-Americans that

00:39:53.760 --> 00:39:56.460
are assimilated, that paradigm.

00:39:56.460 --> 00:40:00.450
And so, it was actually a
sophisticated argument he made,

00:40:00.450 --> 00:40:03.600
that it would be good for the
Republicans in the long run.

00:40:03.600 --> 00:40:07.830
Trying to outbid the
Democrats for social justice

00:40:07.830 --> 00:40:10.740
to the illegal alien
community's not going to work.

00:40:10.740 --> 00:40:12.930
You can't ever
outbid a Democrat.

00:40:12.930 --> 00:40:15.480
But you can say I'm
going to build a wall,

00:40:15.480 --> 00:40:20.220
and then you're going to not be
infused by people who come here

00:40:20.220 --> 00:40:23.040
without education, without
skills, without English,

00:40:23.040 --> 00:40:25.080
and pretty soon you
will be surprised

00:40:25.080 --> 00:40:27.420
how quickly you will
intermarry and assimilate,

00:40:27.420 --> 00:40:30.000
and you will follow patterns
of other ethnic groups.

00:40:30.000 --> 00:40:33.840
And then if your name is
Scalia or Alito or Cuomo,

00:40:33.840 --> 00:40:35.790
we won't know what
your politics are.

00:40:35.790 --> 00:40:37.470
Lopez, Rodriguez,
it won't matter.

00:40:37.470 --> 00:40:38.970
And that's what he
was trying to do,

00:40:38.970 --> 00:40:40.650
and he's got to stick to that.

00:40:40.650 --> 00:40:42.120
And some of it was
iconic, that he

00:40:42.120 --> 00:40:46.290
has to finish some kind of
wall or those people will bolt.

00:40:46.290 --> 00:40:48.824
On trade, he
introduced this idea

00:40:48.824 --> 00:40:49.990
that we've all talked about.

00:40:49.990 --> 00:40:52.740
Fair trade is not free trade.

00:40:52.740 --> 00:40:54.852
The Republican
establishment said--

00:40:54.852 --> 00:40:56.310
I don't know if
they said it, but I

00:40:56.310 --> 00:40:58.830
think a lot of my colleagues
at Hoover would believe it--

00:40:58.830 --> 00:41:03.830
they would say running
up deficits is not bad.

00:41:03.830 --> 00:41:06.770
Foreign competition, even if
it's subsidized on their part,

00:41:06.770 --> 00:41:08.420
is not sustainable.

00:41:08.420 --> 00:41:11.450
It will weaken Germany
or Japan or China.

00:41:11.450 --> 00:41:15.350
And it will make our companies
more leaner and meaner,

00:41:15.350 --> 00:41:17.910
and it will lower prices
at Wal-Mart for everybody.

00:41:17.910 --> 00:41:20.060
It's a great deal.

00:41:20.060 --> 00:41:22.560
And Trump came along and said
you say that because you never

00:41:22.560 --> 00:41:24.590
get laid off.

00:41:24.590 --> 00:41:27.020
As I said earlier, I
don't get a slip that

00:41:27.020 --> 00:41:30.440
says dear Victor Hanson,
a person in Colombia

00:41:30.440 --> 00:41:32.540
wrote a much better
column in the local paper

00:41:32.540 --> 00:41:35.600
and he's going to be hired
at Tribune Media Services.

00:41:35.600 --> 00:41:37.430
So they never suffer
the consequences

00:41:37.430 --> 00:41:39.020
of their own ideology.

00:41:39.020 --> 00:41:40.400
And Trump then appealed.

00:41:40.400 --> 00:41:41.900
I don't think he
had it thought out,

00:41:41.900 --> 00:41:44.960
whether Milton Friedman
economics or not,

00:41:44.960 --> 00:41:48.170
but probably in the long run
is not as good for the economy,

00:41:48.170 --> 00:41:51.500
but he's saying in the short
term, there are victims.

00:41:51.500 --> 00:41:53.720
And we're not going to run
up these trade deficits.

00:41:53.720 --> 00:41:57.110
If they're so great, let
Germany have them for 10 years.

00:41:57.110 --> 00:41:59.311
Let Japan have Milton
Friedman's-- that'll be great

00:41:59.311 --> 00:41:59.810
for them.

00:41:59.810 --> 00:42:00.980
Just try it.

00:42:00.980 --> 00:42:02.490
And see what they say.

00:42:02.490 --> 00:42:06.350
And if it's so great,
why don't they do it?

00:42:06.350 --> 00:42:07.472
Why do we do it?

00:42:07.472 --> 00:42:08.930
And I don't really
care about being

00:42:08.930 --> 00:42:12.830
the leader of the free world if
Youngstown, Ohio is destroyed.

00:42:12.830 --> 00:42:15.380
That was a very
appealing message.

00:42:15.380 --> 00:42:17.810
So he's got to deliver
on that and that

00:42:17.810 --> 00:42:19.940
means he's got to
grow the economy,

00:42:19.940 --> 00:42:25.456
and he's got to keep
unemployment below 5%.

00:42:25.456 --> 00:42:26.330
That's another issue.

00:42:26.330 --> 00:42:29.090
On foreign
interventions, he's got

00:42:29.090 --> 00:42:32.000
to be very careful because he
ran not as an isolationist,

00:42:32.000 --> 00:42:34.570
but as a pragmatic Jacksonian.

00:42:34.570 --> 00:42:37.750
We're nationalists.

00:42:37.750 --> 00:42:38.885
We're whack-a-mole-ists.

00:42:38.885 --> 00:42:40.760
They stick their head
up; we knock them down.

00:42:40.760 --> 00:42:43.280
But we don't put
boots on the ground

00:42:43.280 --> 00:42:45.249
and try to change
hearts and minds.

00:42:45.249 --> 00:42:47.540
I happen to think that Iraq
eventually probably worked.

00:42:47.540 --> 00:42:49.873
It would have been like Korea
if we hadn't precipitously

00:42:49.873 --> 00:42:51.710
pulled out in 2011.

00:42:51.710 --> 00:42:55.190
Sort of like Eisenhower
coming up for reelection in 56

00:42:55.190 --> 00:42:58.640
and said Truman put those guys
on the 38th parallel, not me.

00:42:58.640 --> 00:43:00.260
I'm pulling them out.

00:43:00.260 --> 00:43:01.700
For a cheap campaign
talk, I don't

00:43:01.700 --> 00:43:03.980
think we'd have Kia
and Samsung today.

00:43:03.980 --> 00:43:06.820
But nevertheless, that's
not Trump's interest.

00:43:06.820 --> 00:43:09.320
His trump is to be punitive.

00:43:09.320 --> 00:43:10.892
And yet, Afghanistan is a mess.

00:43:10.892 --> 00:43:12.350
If we pull out,
there's going to be

00:43:12.350 --> 00:43:16.160
a lot of people butchered there,
sort of like South Vietnam

00:43:16.160 --> 00:43:17.620
circa 1975.

00:43:17.620 --> 00:43:20.000
So, yet, if he
puts 5,000 troops,

00:43:20.000 --> 00:43:22.130
and we get involved
more and more,

00:43:22.130 --> 00:43:25.004
that group of people
that he promised

00:43:25.004 --> 00:43:26.420
that he wouldn't
do that are going

00:43:26.420 --> 00:43:27.860
to be sorely disappointed.

00:43:27.860 --> 00:43:29.930
Same thing with some of
these trade agreements,

00:43:29.930 --> 00:43:33.050
like the Pacific trade
agreement or NAFTA.

00:43:33.050 --> 00:43:36.180
He's got to at least bring
back something to the table.

00:43:36.180 --> 00:43:37.820
And they're very
flexible, but if they

00:43:37.820 --> 00:43:40.910
feel that he's a typical
politician that promises

00:43:40.910 --> 00:43:43.730
something and then he flips
back and he joins the Jeb Bush

00:43:43.730 --> 00:43:45.710
establishment, he's
not going to be

00:43:45.710 --> 00:43:49.710
successful in those eight or
nine states that he has to win.

00:43:49.710 --> 00:43:52.470
Because that margin
is very, very thin.

00:43:52.470 --> 00:43:55.020
And if he loses
150,000 votes here,

00:43:55.020 --> 00:43:56.940
he's not going to be reelected.

00:43:56.940 --> 00:44:01.710
If he does what I suggested--
that is not my idea,

00:44:01.710 --> 00:44:04.040
but if he's true to these
promises to these voters--

00:44:04.040 --> 00:44:05.055
he will win.

00:44:05.055 --> 00:44:06.680
And it's going to
change the Republican

00:44:06.680 --> 00:44:08.150
Party if he gets re-elected.

00:44:08.150 --> 00:44:09.860
It already has changed.

00:44:09.860 --> 00:44:12.470
The Republican Party is
now a more populous party,

00:44:12.470 --> 00:44:14.240
and it's a more
empathetic party, I think.

00:44:14.240 --> 00:44:19.602
And I like this idea that nobody
knows who to listen to anymore.

00:44:19.602 --> 00:44:21.560
I like the idea that
writers at the Wall Street

00:44:21.560 --> 00:44:23.930
Journal, National Review,
or Weekly Standard or Comey

00:44:23.930 --> 00:44:25.910
are not gospel.

00:44:25.910 --> 00:44:28.220
That there are people
coming in online.

00:44:28.220 --> 00:44:29.362
It's just a free for all.

00:44:29.362 --> 00:44:30.570
There's no hierarchy anymore.

00:44:30.570 --> 00:44:32.900
I think this is
much more healthy

00:44:32.900 --> 00:44:35.870
than this geriatric cadre
that runs the Democratic Party

00:44:35.870 --> 00:44:37.100
or Hollywood--

00:44:37.100 --> 00:44:40.910
intolerant and almost Stalinist.

00:44:40.910 --> 00:44:43.250
Let me just finish by
the Democratic Party.

00:44:43.250 --> 00:44:45.545
They have a choice, don't they?

00:44:45.545 --> 00:44:51.910
They can be empirical and see
that if Hillary Clinton had

00:44:51.910 --> 00:44:56.350
appealed to the working classes
and moderated her rhetoric,

00:44:56.350 --> 00:45:00.460
not talk about irredeemables,
not sold herself out

00:45:00.460 --> 00:45:03.010
to the identity
politics movement,

00:45:03.010 --> 00:45:05.359
she probably would not have
lost too many of her base

00:45:05.359 --> 00:45:06.650
and she would have gained some.

00:45:06.650 --> 00:45:10.870
And she did win 3 million
plus voters, by a margin.

00:45:10.870 --> 00:45:13.360
She probably would have come
close in only the one or two

00:45:13.360 --> 00:45:16.120
states she needed to do, and
she probably would have won.

00:45:16.120 --> 00:45:19.610
Any sane person would see
that would be the anecdote.

00:45:19.610 --> 00:45:22.660
So what you would
not want to do is

00:45:22.660 --> 00:45:27.220
nominate somebody like Keith
Ellison or Perez, Tom Perez,

00:45:27.220 --> 00:45:29.830
as the DNC chairman.

00:45:29.830 --> 00:45:33.430
Or you would not want to
continue this, call it,

00:45:33.430 --> 00:45:36.820
the resistance, as if these
people are French fighters out

00:45:36.820 --> 00:45:40.090
in the Maquis, trying
to ambush the Waffen SS.

00:45:40.090 --> 00:45:41.350
It's not a good image.

00:45:41.350 --> 00:45:46.090
It's like 1968, when
the Democratic Party

00:45:46.090 --> 00:45:49.650
came within 3/10 of a
percentage point of beating

00:45:49.650 --> 00:45:52.640
the very unpopular, more
unpopular than Trump,

00:45:52.640 --> 00:45:54.400
Richard Nixon.

00:45:54.400 --> 00:45:57.280
And they were killed
because George Wallace took

00:45:57.280 --> 00:46:01.060
13% of the vote, which
translated in the loss of five

00:46:01.060 --> 00:46:05.440
southern states to
Wallace, 5 to Nixon,

00:46:05.440 --> 00:46:06.700
that Humphrey would have won.

00:46:06.700 --> 00:46:08.440
And then you add
in the left wing

00:46:08.440 --> 00:46:12.190
lunacy at the
Chicago convention,

00:46:12.190 --> 00:46:13.510
and they blew that election.

00:46:13.510 --> 00:46:15.880
And then the anecdote
was obviously

00:46:15.880 --> 00:46:20.890
to tell somebody like
Humphrey or Muske or somebody,

00:46:20.890 --> 00:46:23.980
don't get caught with an
anti-war fringe group.

00:46:23.980 --> 00:46:26.910
And make sure that Wallace,
don't shoot him, but make sure

00:46:26.910 --> 00:46:30.040
he doesn't run in '72, which
he did, and he was shot,

00:46:30.040 --> 00:46:32.620
and he was not a
successful candidate.

00:46:32.620 --> 00:46:34.460
But try to get
that working class.

00:46:34.460 --> 00:46:35.860
And what did they do?

00:46:35.860 --> 00:46:40.400
They said '71 Voters Act gave
18-year-olds the right to vote.

00:46:40.400 --> 00:46:42.530
This is the sort of new
and progressive movement.

00:46:42.530 --> 00:46:44.740
We're going to get
George McGovern and win.

00:46:44.740 --> 00:46:48.550
It was third largest landslide
in American electoral history.

00:46:48.550 --> 00:46:50.680
Because they doubled
down on failure,

00:46:50.680 --> 00:46:54.340
and it took them two elections
to realize that nobody gets

00:46:54.340 --> 00:46:57.250
nominated who wins after JFK.

00:46:57.250 --> 00:47:01.330
Between JFK and Barack Obama
were both different candidates,

00:47:01.330 --> 00:47:04.360
nobody could win unless
they had a southern accent.

00:47:04.360 --> 00:47:06.760
Nominate a guy like Al Gore,
he wins the popular vote.

00:47:06.760 --> 00:47:08.093
Bill Clinton wins the president.

00:47:08.093 --> 00:47:10.750
Jimmy Carter wins, and LBJ.

00:47:10.750 --> 00:47:14.560
Nominate somebody like
Mondale or Dukakis or Kerry,

00:47:14.560 --> 00:47:15.800
they don't win.

00:47:15.800 --> 00:47:17.800
Because people didn't
trust the Democratic Party

00:47:17.800 --> 00:47:19.174
on social issues,
and they wanted

00:47:19.174 --> 00:47:21.970
some authentic
conservative, they

00:47:21.970 --> 00:47:23.829
thought, with a southern accent.

00:47:23.829 --> 00:47:25.120
So that was sort of the remedy.

00:47:25.120 --> 00:47:26.890
And Obama brought
this new paradigm.

00:47:26.890 --> 00:47:28.790
It's not, as I said,
not transferable.

00:47:28.790 --> 00:47:30.430
So they have a choice to make.

00:47:30.430 --> 00:47:33.700
If you don't want
to see them elected,

00:47:33.700 --> 00:47:37.510
then I hope you're very happy
that Stephen Colbert and Bill

00:47:37.510 --> 00:47:41.770
Maher are using this
atrocious language,

00:47:41.770 --> 00:47:44.560
that we see who's running
the DNC are pretty much,

00:47:44.560 --> 00:47:48.100
they can't say a speech
without using profanity.

00:47:48.100 --> 00:47:51.670
And Hollywood's enlisted
Madonna, Ashley Judd.

00:47:51.670 --> 00:47:55.060
All of these people are building
this image that we saw in '72,

00:47:55.060 --> 00:47:57.850
and it leads nowhere
but to oblivion.

00:47:57.850 --> 00:47:59.680
And so Trump has been
given a great gift

00:47:59.680 --> 00:48:01.690
in the nature of his opposition.

00:48:01.690 --> 00:48:05.410
So basically if Trump
can please his base

00:48:05.410 --> 00:48:10.300
and grow the economy at 3% and
avoid a major war by restoring

00:48:10.300 --> 00:48:12.340
deterrence, he's going to win.

00:48:12.340 --> 00:48:17.050
And if he doesn't,
he's still going

00:48:17.050 --> 00:48:20.097
to win if the Democratic
Party goes the McGovern route.

00:48:20.097 --> 00:48:20.930
Thank you very much.

00:48:20.930 --> 00:48:22.055
If you have any questions--

00:48:22.055 --> 00:48:36.903
[APPLAUSE]

00:48:42.840 --> 00:48:45.280
AUDIENCE: My name is Howard.

00:48:45.280 --> 00:48:48.490
About 50 years ago,
Nikita Khrushchev

00:48:48.490 --> 00:48:52.000
came to the United Nations
in a very famous news clip.

00:48:52.000 --> 00:48:55.720
He took his shoe off
and pounded the table.

00:48:55.720 --> 00:48:58.600
One interpretation of
that was that Khrushchev

00:48:58.600 --> 00:49:04.270
wanted to show that he was crazy
enough to possibly use nukes.

00:49:04.270 --> 00:49:10.210
Is it possible that Mr. Trump's
sometimes irrational tweets

00:49:10.210 --> 00:49:14.027
have the same kind of effect
on our international enemies?

00:49:14.027 --> 00:49:16.360
VICTOR DAVIS HANSON: No, I
think it's not only possible;

00:49:16.360 --> 00:49:18.880
it's almost guaranteed.

00:49:18.880 --> 00:49:22.350
If you read-- it is painful,
but read The Art of the Deal

00:49:22.350 --> 00:49:24.700
or The Art of The Comeback
or Making America Great,

00:49:24.700 --> 00:49:28.120
and you ask for twice
as much as you expect.

00:49:28.120 --> 00:49:29.350
You get 51%.

00:49:29.350 --> 00:49:30.910
That's a great deal.

00:49:30.910 --> 00:49:33.370
You act crazy so you
unnerve your opponent.

00:49:33.370 --> 00:49:35.980
We've always known, and
Henry Kissinger wrote that

00:49:35.980 --> 00:49:37.360
in his book about deterrence.

00:49:37.360 --> 00:49:40.270
Acting unpredictable
was an advantage.

00:49:40.270 --> 00:49:43.240
Remember in the Yom Kippur War
Nixon said, no, you go over

00:49:43.240 --> 00:49:46.120
and tell the Russians that
I'm crazy, I'm a mad bomber,

00:49:46.120 --> 00:49:48.516
and I'm going to put us
on stage three alert.

00:49:48.516 --> 00:49:50.390
And then you're the
sober and judicious voice

00:49:50.390 --> 00:49:52.931
that has to calm me down, and
you'll have a lot more success.

00:49:52.931 --> 00:49:54.760
Nixon said that, and it worked.

00:49:54.760 --> 00:49:56.920
And so I think
that's sort of what--

00:49:56.920 --> 00:49:58.420
I don't think they
want to broadcast

00:49:58.420 --> 00:50:03.730
that-- but I when I see McMaster
on Sunday evening TV or Sunday

00:50:03.730 --> 00:50:05.860
morning, I see
Mattis, it's almost

00:50:05.860 --> 00:50:08.410
like they're telling
the world, you guys

00:50:08.410 --> 00:50:10.280
don't know what we
have to put up with.

00:50:10.280 --> 00:50:12.700
We're dealing with a guy who
wants to get out of NATO.

00:50:12.700 --> 00:50:14.500
He wants to nuke Pyongyang.

00:50:14.500 --> 00:50:15.410
He'll do anything.

00:50:15.410 --> 00:50:16.780
But you've got to deal with us.

00:50:16.780 --> 00:50:18.070
Help me out.

00:50:18.070 --> 00:50:19.810
And then you almost
get the impression

00:50:19.810 --> 00:50:21.310
that they go into
a room with Trump,

00:50:21.310 --> 00:50:24.610
and Trump says, well
how did it go, you guys?

00:50:24.610 --> 00:50:28.750
And so, the danger of that
is that our adversaries

00:50:28.750 --> 00:50:29.740
are brilliant people.

00:50:29.740 --> 00:50:32.500
And they're trying to say
Trump and those guys--

00:50:32.500 --> 00:50:33.940
this is a script
of The Apprentice

00:50:33.940 --> 00:50:35.410
and don't fall for it.

00:50:35.410 --> 00:50:37.814
So we have to at
some point deliver.

00:50:37.814 --> 00:50:39.480
And so that's a danger
that we all have,

00:50:39.480 --> 00:50:42.251
and I think you're
absolutely right.

00:50:42.251 --> 00:50:42.750
Yes.

00:50:50.160 --> 00:50:52.037
AUDIENCE: Thank you, professor.

00:50:52.037 --> 00:50:52.870
Thank you very much.

00:50:52.870 --> 00:50:54.840
Michael Maybock.

00:50:54.840 --> 00:51:00.420
In the case of North
Korea, for lots of us,

00:51:00.420 --> 00:51:04.410
it seems like it's an impossible
situation because their troops

00:51:04.410 --> 00:51:06.720
are so close to Seoul.

00:51:06.720 --> 00:51:11.160
And so even though he
sends the 6 fleet etc.

00:51:11.160 --> 00:51:15.660
At some point does that
become a real problem for him

00:51:15.660 --> 00:51:20.360
to use leverage?

00:51:20.360 --> 00:51:23.360
VICTOR DAVIS HANSON:
Well, it's very tricky,

00:51:23.360 --> 00:51:25.750
but I don't think any
situation throughout history

00:51:25.750 --> 00:51:26.510
is impossible.

00:51:26.510 --> 00:51:28.340
There's always an answer.

00:51:28.340 --> 00:51:32.750
And North Korea realized
that when you act crazy,

00:51:32.750 --> 00:51:35.450
and you have nothing to lose,
and you have a few nukes,

00:51:35.450 --> 00:51:39.322
then that results in bribery.

00:51:39.322 --> 00:51:41.030
They've got billions
of dollars, and Iran

00:51:41.030 --> 00:51:42.238
is following the same script.

00:51:42.238 --> 00:51:44.350
Pakistan does the same thing.

00:51:44.350 --> 00:51:47.300
And that when you're sober and
judicious like Israel or France

00:51:47.300 --> 00:51:49.370
you get nothing with nukes.

00:51:49.370 --> 00:51:51.290
So we all understand the
game they're playing.

00:51:51.290 --> 00:51:52.910
The question is
can we find a way

00:51:52.910 --> 00:51:56.840
to mitigate it without
leading to the loss of Seoul?

00:51:56.840 --> 00:51:59.990
The first thing we have to do
is get on board with our allies.

00:51:59.990 --> 00:52:03.860
We have to get Japan and Taiwan
and Australia and South Korea,

00:52:03.860 --> 00:52:06.510
Philippines, all
on the same page.

00:52:06.510 --> 00:52:10.430
So I don't object to him having
the Philippine president there

00:52:10.430 --> 00:52:11.989
who's kind of thuggish anymore.

00:52:11.989 --> 00:52:14.030
I mean he's not as thuggish
as the Iran president

00:52:14.030 --> 00:52:15.190
that we dealt with.

00:52:15.190 --> 00:52:16.940
So you've got to get
him on the same page.

00:52:16.940 --> 00:52:19.730
You can't have South Korea
freelancing and saying

00:52:19.730 --> 00:52:23.330
to the north, these mean
Americans are pushing us.

00:52:23.330 --> 00:52:26.370
They can't be doing that,
and they're doing that now

00:52:26.370 --> 00:52:27.750
with this new president.

00:52:27.750 --> 00:52:29.730
The second thing is
that we do have--

00:52:29.730 --> 00:52:32.190
as Trump has showed--

00:52:32.190 --> 00:52:35.040
Trump has been criticized
because, like a madman,

00:52:35.040 --> 00:52:38.690
he says China is
rigging the currency.

00:52:38.690 --> 00:52:40.470
China's running up debt.

00:52:40.470 --> 00:52:42.425
China's cheating on patents.

00:52:42.425 --> 00:52:43.800
And then he's
quietly saying, now

00:52:43.800 --> 00:52:47.590
if you're willing to help
us pressure them, cut off

00:52:47.590 --> 00:52:49.350
their coal imports,
or what, then we're

00:52:49.350 --> 00:52:50.970
going to drop all that.

00:52:50.970 --> 00:52:54.544
Well, in Trump's mind, if
they help on North Korea,

00:52:54.544 --> 00:52:56.460
then we're back where
we started economically.

00:52:56.460 --> 00:52:59.410
No worse off, but we're a lot
better off with North Korea.

00:52:59.410 --> 00:53:03.570
So that's the only
country they can pressure

00:53:03.570 --> 00:53:05.040
because they have
a billion people,

00:53:05.040 --> 00:53:07.123
and I don't think North
Korea thinks they're going

00:53:07.123 --> 00:53:08.760
to do much damage to China.

00:53:08.760 --> 00:53:11.999
So China has been
playing a dirty game.

00:53:11.999 --> 00:53:14.040
It's sort of like having
a pit bull with a leash,

00:53:14.040 --> 00:53:16.248
and every once in a while
they say, where's my leash?

00:53:16.248 --> 00:53:18.000
My pit bull's
missing, and they know

00:53:18.000 --> 00:53:20.040
it goes right into your
yard and bites you.

00:53:20.040 --> 00:53:21.780
I had a neighbor
kind of like that.

00:53:21.780 --> 00:53:24.220
He'd always say, I don't
know where my pit bulls are.

00:53:24.220 --> 00:53:27.120
And anytime we had an argument,
they ended up in my yard

00:53:27.120 --> 00:53:28.440
biting me.

00:53:28.440 --> 00:53:30.550
So that's what North Korea does.

00:53:30.550 --> 00:53:33.360
And we have to say, you
can't do that anymore.

00:53:33.360 --> 00:53:35.670
And if you do it,
you're going to face

00:53:35.670 --> 00:53:37.500
severe economic ramifications.

00:53:37.500 --> 00:53:41.490
And we have a final
and unsavory card,

00:53:41.490 --> 00:53:44.940
and I think that Donald
Rumsfeld played it once in 2006.

00:53:44.940 --> 00:53:49.786
We tell China and North Korea
the world is a very dangerous

00:53:49.786 --> 00:53:50.910
place in your neighborhood.

00:53:50.910 --> 00:53:51.910
Mexico is not nuclear.

00:53:51.910 --> 00:53:53.100
Canada is not nuclear.

00:53:53.100 --> 00:53:54.510
We have two oceans.

00:53:54.510 --> 00:53:59.340
I know that's nuclear war, but
we have some preparatory time.

00:53:59.340 --> 00:54:01.140
But you've got nuclear Pakistan.

00:54:01.140 --> 00:54:02.320
You've got nuclear Russia.

00:54:02.320 --> 00:54:03.660
You've got nuclear India.

00:54:03.660 --> 00:54:06.120
You've got American
nuclear assets.

00:54:06.120 --> 00:54:08.660
You've got nuclear Iran.

00:54:08.660 --> 00:54:12.150
Do you really want a nuclear
Japan, Taiwan, and South Korea,

00:54:12.150 --> 00:54:13.980
and Australia around you?

00:54:13.980 --> 00:54:16.060
Because you're headed that way.

00:54:16.060 --> 00:54:19.260
Because they're going to need
deterrence, and our clients'

00:54:19.260 --> 00:54:22.820
nuclear weapons will work
like Kias or Toyotas.

00:54:22.820 --> 00:54:25.890
They won't roll up on the
launching pad like yours.

00:54:25.890 --> 00:54:29.400
So you broke the agreement
that your client would not

00:54:29.400 --> 00:54:31.290
go nuclear, and we're
going to break ours,

00:54:31.290 --> 00:54:34.090
and they can go nuclear
if they wish to.

00:54:34.090 --> 00:54:35.400
That's about all we have.

00:54:38.280 --> 00:54:41.040
I hate to say, if you're
going to insist on sanctions

00:54:41.040 --> 00:54:43.406
with North Korea and then
people are eating grass,

00:54:43.406 --> 00:54:45.780
you can't be like the Clinton
administration-- oh my God,

00:54:45.780 --> 00:54:47.370
they're eating grass.

00:54:47.370 --> 00:54:49.680
Well, they have to eat grass
for the sanctions to work.

00:54:49.680 --> 00:54:51.960
I hate to say it, but
it's better than people

00:54:51.960 --> 00:54:53.690
getting nuked.

00:54:53.690 --> 00:54:59.750
So we have these limited, but
necessary and valuable options.

00:54:59.750 --> 00:55:02.420
I think they're all
being played right now.

00:55:02.420 --> 00:55:03.350
And I hope they work.

00:55:06.970 --> 00:55:09.850
AUDIENCE: Thanks, and
thank you for your speech.

00:55:09.850 --> 00:55:12.760
I was just wondering, you were
talking earlier about 2016,

00:55:12.760 --> 00:55:15.790
and how a lot of Democrats
really didn't think

00:55:15.790 --> 00:55:17.470
that Trump could win at all.

00:55:17.470 --> 00:55:20.140
And in fact, the media was
constantly talking about that.

00:55:20.140 --> 00:55:23.890
I also noticed that they did
that in 2012 with Romney,

00:55:23.890 --> 00:55:25.630
and I was very
suspicious of that

00:55:25.630 --> 00:55:27.160
because I never
quite understood why

00:55:27.160 --> 00:55:30.400
Romney was so far in the hole
that he couldn't possibly win.

00:55:30.400 --> 00:55:32.980
It's one thing to say
he's the underdog;

00:55:32.980 --> 00:55:35.020
it's another thing
to say he can't win.

00:55:35.020 --> 00:55:37.572
And they did the same
thing with Trump.

00:55:37.572 --> 00:55:39.280
And I'm wondering, do
you think that it's

00:55:39.280 --> 00:55:41.290
possible this is just
a strategy they're

00:55:41.290 --> 00:55:42.804
going to keep trying to use?

00:55:42.804 --> 00:55:45.220
VICTOR DAVIS HANSON: Well,
obviously it's of some utility,

00:55:45.220 --> 00:55:47.219
yes, because then they
tell their voters there's

00:55:47.219 --> 00:55:49.457
no reason to go out and vote.

00:55:49.457 --> 00:55:51.040
They're sort of like
the San Francisco

00:55:51.040 --> 00:55:52.082
Giants in a losing year.

00:55:52.082 --> 00:55:53.290
Why would you want to go out?

00:55:53.290 --> 00:55:54.070
You're not going to win.

00:55:54.070 --> 00:55:55.600
Why would you want
to go watch them?

00:55:55.600 --> 00:55:57.420
Don't participate
in a losing cause.

00:55:57.420 --> 00:55:59.560
So yes, I think that
was a lot of it.

00:55:59.560 --> 00:56:02.590
But also, they believed,
after eight years

00:56:02.590 --> 00:56:05.260
of this sort of yuppie
identity politics,

00:56:05.260 --> 00:56:09.640
that this was the trajectory
where the Health Care Act was

00:56:09.640 --> 00:56:12.640
a prelude to the single payer.

00:56:12.640 --> 00:56:16.310
Black Lives Matter was a
prelude to gay marriage

00:56:16.310 --> 00:56:19.490
to transgender restrooms
to who knows what next,

00:56:19.490 --> 00:56:22.300
no gender identification
on their driver's license.

00:56:22.300 --> 00:56:24.940
Everything was a
logical, incremental step

00:56:24.940 --> 00:56:26.770
to a progressive paradise.

00:56:26.770 --> 00:56:27.480
And it was fated.

00:56:27.480 --> 00:56:29.170
It was Hegelian.

00:56:29.170 --> 00:56:30.940
It was fated.

00:56:30.940 --> 00:56:32.650
And that's what they believed.

00:56:32.650 --> 00:56:36.940
And they thought that the
white working class was

00:56:36.940 --> 00:56:40.090
self-destructive
perhaps, suicidal maybe,

00:56:40.090 --> 00:56:45.160
of short longevity, we
know that, and doomed.

00:56:45.160 --> 00:56:47.800
And so they were going to
make the necessary political

00:56:47.800 --> 00:56:49.508
adjustments, and they
were going to have,

00:56:49.508 --> 00:56:53.500
I think 50 years, some people-
and they miscalculated,

00:56:53.500 --> 00:56:55.150
terribly miscalculated.

00:56:55.150 --> 00:56:57.910
Because they didn't understand
the electoral college.

00:56:57.910 --> 00:57:00.730
They didn't understand
that not everybody believes

00:57:00.730 --> 00:57:03.150
that your race is your destiny.

00:57:03.150 --> 00:57:07.100
There is a growing number
of people in my family,

00:57:07.100 --> 00:57:09.520
to take one example
of a mixed race,

00:57:09.520 --> 00:57:11.680
and they don't
identify with any race,

00:57:11.680 --> 00:57:14.560
and people want to be Americans.

00:57:14.560 --> 00:57:19.610
And so the identity politics
is not necessarily our destiny.

00:57:19.610 --> 00:57:21.350
They misinterpret
a lot of things.

00:57:21.350 --> 00:57:24.130
But give them credit, they
won by 3 million votes,

00:57:24.130 --> 00:57:26.710
and they almost won
the electoral college.

00:57:26.710 --> 00:57:29.710
It was about 250,000
brave Americans

00:57:29.710 --> 00:57:32.260
that said I've had enough
and went out and voted

00:57:32.260 --> 00:57:35.470
for the first time, and
they voted against Hillary.

00:57:35.470 --> 00:57:39.010
And you've got to give
Donald Trump credit because,

00:57:39.010 --> 00:57:41.310
for someone who
had no political--

00:57:41.310 --> 00:57:44.830
the most unique phenomenon in
American electoral history.

00:57:44.830 --> 00:57:46.810
Somebody with no
political experience.

00:57:46.810 --> 00:57:49.690
First president that neither
had a political office

00:57:49.690 --> 00:57:54.280
holding or military office
rank won the presidency.

00:57:54.280 --> 00:57:57.070
And before Bannon
and before Conway

00:57:57.070 --> 00:57:59.440
and before all of his
strategists came on,

00:57:59.440 --> 00:58:01.810
he crafted that strategy.

00:58:01.810 --> 00:58:03.740
You can say that
he just made it up.

00:58:03.740 --> 00:58:05.950
But he crafted the
strategy that none

00:58:05.950 --> 00:58:11.590
of his gifted Republican
contingents and opponents

00:58:11.590 --> 00:58:12.250
actualized.

00:58:12.250 --> 00:58:14.672
It was quite
brilliant what he did.

00:58:14.672 --> 00:58:15.880
I was telling a group today--

00:58:15.880 --> 00:58:17.450
I keep saying because
I've talked to a group.

00:58:17.450 --> 00:58:19.783
I don't want to repeat myself
if you're in the audience,

00:58:19.783 --> 00:58:27.220
but I have a wife who's, I
would say, middle of the road.

00:58:27.220 --> 00:58:30.610
And she said to me, you've
got to come in here.

00:58:30.610 --> 00:58:33.790
This man is-- I'm going
to vote for Donald Trump.

00:58:33.790 --> 00:58:35.110
I said why?

00:58:35.110 --> 00:58:36.370
And she said come and look.

00:58:36.370 --> 00:58:39.940
And what she was referring
to was Jorge Ramos,

00:58:39.940 --> 00:58:42.790
Univision, who's a
multimillionaire fled

00:58:42.790 --> 00:58:45.310
to the safety the United
States, cashed in big,

00:58:45.310 --> 00:58:49.030
lives in a gated community, put
his kids in private schools,

00:58:49.030 --> 00:58:51.460
daughters working
for Hillary Clinton

00:58:51.460 --> 00:58:53.650
and says that Trump is a
racist and our country is

00:58:53.650 --> 00:58:56.890
racist for voting for him, tried
to crash that press conference.

00:58:56.890 --> 00:58:58.840
Interrupted a number of times.

00:58:58.840 --> 00:59:03.020
And then Trump-- she said Donald
Trump deported Jorge Ramos

00:59:03.020 --> 00:59:04.390
from his press conference.

00:59:04.390 --> 00:59:06.070
He said, get out of here.

00:59:06.070 --> 00:59:08.230
Nobody's ever done
that to that guy.

00:59:08.230 --> 00:59:10.720
Doesn't he know what
they're going to do to him?

00:59:10.720 --> 00:59:12.409
I said, he doesn't care.

00:59:12.409 --> 00:59:14.200
And she goes, no, he
doesn't care, does he?

00:59:14.200 --> 00:59:15.782
I'm going to vote for him.

00:59:15.782 --> 00:59:17.740
And I think a lot of
people had that visceral--

00:59:17.740 --> 00:59:19.990
I don't know if that's a
paradigm of governance,

00:59:19.990 --> 00:59:22.910
but it surely electrified
people when he did that.

00:59:22.910 --> 00:59:25.450
He pointed out and said
look at those media people.

00:59:25.450 --> 00:59:26.710
They're all liars.

00:59:26.710 --> 00:59:29.860
And I thought, well, I'm
going to read tomorrow,

00:59:29.860 --> 00:59:32.500
this wrinkle frown
op-ed that this

00:59:32.500 --> 00:59:35.050
is a threat to our republic.

00:59:35.050 --> 00:59:38.620
But when Glenn Thrush
writes to John Podesta

00:59:38.620 --> 00:59:41.710
and says I'm a hack, we want
to proofread my article,

00:59:41.710 --> 00:59:44.710
or Dana Milbank says I'm going
to attack Trump tomorrow,

00:59:44.710 --> 00:59:46.870
can you guys give me
some free research?

00:59:46.870 --> 00:59:48.695
That's what they are.

00:59:48.695 --> 00:59:49.570
That's what they are.

00:59:49.570 --> 00:59:51.730
Or the New York Times says you
can't be disinterested anymore

00:59:51.730 --> 00:59:53.521
because Trump is unique,
and therefore, you

00:59:53.521 --> 00:59:54.730
have to be at the source.

00:59:54.730 --> 01:00:00.840
So he's crude, but he always
hits the essence of it.

01:00:00.840 --> 01:00:02.780
I have no idea what
is sustainable or not.

01:00:10.090 --> 01:00:13.422
AUDIENCE: I've been
watching the media reaction

01:00:13.422 --> 01:00:14.880
with a certain
amount of amusement,

01:00:14.880 --> 01:00:16.713
because I have to tell
you, it's like dinner

01:00:16.713 --> 01:00:18.710
and a show every night.

01:00:18.710 --> 01:00:21.366
And I'm kind of curious--

01:00:21.366 --> 01:00:22.990
there's an awful lot
of people like me.

01:00:22.990 --> 01:00:24.720
I just quit watching the news.

01:00:24.720 --> 01:00:26.400
I get my news in other ways.

01:00:26.400 --> 01:00:27.960
I get my news
through online blogs.

01:00:27.960 --> 01:00:28.830
I find other things.

01:00:28.830 --> 01:00:30.150
I find other ways to do it.

01:00:30.150 --> 01:00:31.436
Where do you see this going?

01:00:31.436 --> 01:00:32.310
I mean do you think--

01:00:32.310 --> 01:00:34.601
VICTOR DAVIS HANSON: I think
what you're referring to--

01:00:34.601 --> 01:00:37.140
I wrote an article once called
"The Monastery of the Mind,"

01:00:37.140 --> 01:00:39.850
where we've all retreated
to mental monasteries.

01:00:39.850 --> 01:00:42.060
And I mentioned this earlier.

01:00:42.060 --> 01:00:43.440
I used to watch ESPN.

01:00:43.440 --> 01:00:44.280
I like sports.

01:00:44.280 --> 01:00:47.400
But I don't like to have a jock
lecture me on my politics who's

01:00:47.400 --> 01:00:48.630
an ignoramus.

01:00:48.630 --> 01:00:50.310
So I just tuned the
whole thing out.

01:00:50.310 --> 01:00:52.440
And then I noticed,
there must be a lot of us

01:00:52.440 --> 01:00:54.840
because ESPN is almost broke.

01:00:54.840 --> 01:00:59.444
And then I noticed that
about three years ago, I

01:00:59.444 --> 01:01:00.110
went to a movie.

01:01:00.110 --> 01:01:00.920
I didn't know what it was.

01:01:00.920 --> 01:01:02.378
I was shopping,
and I just thought,

01:01:02.378 --> 01:01:06.080
well, it's going to be either
a diversity person is going

01:01:06.080 --> 01:01:08.310
to be attacking an
evil corporation

01:01:08.310 --> 01:01:10.200
or there's going to be
evil people with South

01:01:10.200 --> 01:01:12.800
African or southern accents.

01:01:12.800 --> 01:01:14.050
And sure enough, it was.

01:01:14.050 --> 01:01:16.430
Or it's going to be a
remake of a great movie,

01:01:16.430 --> 01:01:19.970
and it's going to be
a terrible remake.

01:01:19.970 --> 01:01:23.600
I just watched a great movie,
this Magnificent Seven,

01:01:23.600 --> 01:01:25.760
and I went to watch the
remake and it was diverse,

01:01:25.760 --> 01:01:27.020
it was horrible.

01:01:27.020 --> 01:01:30.140
So I thought to myself, I'm
not going to go to the movies

01:01:30.140 --> 01:01:30.757
anymore.

01:01:30.757 --> 01:01:31.590
And I quit doing it.

01:01:31.590 --> 01:01:34.340
And I noticed that
everybody's quit doing it.

01:01:34.340 --> 01:01:36.780
And I don't mean everybody,
but half the population.

01:01:36.780 --> 01:01:38.946
And I think that's what's
happening, that people are

01:01:38.946 --> 01:01:41.810
psychologically disengaging.

01:01:41.810 --> 01:01:44.700
There was a word in classical
Greek called sophrosyne

01:01:44.700 --> 01:01:48.530
and it meant not just prudence,
but prudence in the sense

01:01:48.530 --> 01:01:50.900
that you didn't want
to participate anymore

01:01:50.900 --> 01:01:54.190
in the arena of a radical
Athenian democracy.

01:01:54.190 --> 01:01:57.000
A guy wrote a book called
The Quiet Athenian.

01:01:57.000 --> 01:01:59.892
It's about people who just
sort of disengaged from Athens.

01:01:59.892 --> 01:02:01.850
Because they thought they
had nothing to offer,

01:02:01.850 --> 01:02:03.860
and they were not appreciated.

01:02:03.860 --> 01:02:06.380
And in California, I
see them everywhere.

01:02:06.380 --> 01:02:10.780
As one person just told
me, I don't mind paying 13%

01:02:10.780 --> 01:02:14.910
and still getting a 46th
ranked schools and 49th roads,

01:02:14.910 --> 01:02:16.720
but I don't like
it when I'm called

01:02:16.720 --> 01:02:21.790
greedy and avaricious and
selfish for paying 13%.

01:02:21.790 --> 01:02:24.250
So I think a lot of us have
disengaged from the media

01:02:24.250 --> 01:02:29.230
and we're looking for
outlets or alternative news.

01:02:29.230 --> 01:02:30.580
And I think it's healthy.

01:02:30.580 --> 01:02:33.470
Again, I think this
chaos is wonderful.

01:02:33.470 --> 01:02:37.250
And I'm a dropout like
you on a lot of things.

01:02:37.250 --> 01:02:40.310
I don't watch the
Super Bowl anymore.

01:02:40.310 --> 01:02:42.620
If there's a concert
on TV and it's

01:02:42.620 --> 01:02:46.026
Madonna, any popular
entertainment, I turn it off.

01:02:46.026 --> 01:02:47.150
I think I wrote about this.

01:02:47.150 --> 01:02:49.940
I went to a service
station on the Chico pass

01:02:49.940 --> 01:02:52.040
when I was going back
from Stanford home,

01:02:52.040 --> 01:02:55.220
and a rapper pulled
up next to me,

01:02:55.220 --> 01:02:59.300
and he had this typical rap--
kill the bitch, shoot the pig,

01:02:59.300 --> 01:03:00.120
really loud.

01:03:00.120 --> 01:03:03.440
And I was just, and I thought,
I'm not going to take this.

01:03:03.440 --> 01:03:04.460
I'm 63 years old.

01:03:04.460 --> 01:03:06.887
So I had two songs.

01:03:06.887 --> 01:03:08.470
I had a truck, and
it took me a while,

01:03:08.470 --> 01:03:13.300
so I had that famous
folk song, I think.

01:03:13.300 --> 01:03:15.340
I don't know who wrote it.

01:03:15.340 --> 01:03:17.860
But it was called
"The Colorado Trail."

01:03:17.860 --> 01:03:18.950
It's a cowboy song.

01:03:18.950 --> 01:03:23.280
I turned that on
as loud as I could.

01:03:23.280 --> 01:03:25.090
And then I turned--

01:03:25.090 --> 01:03:30.220
the next channel was Joan
Baez, "The Pleasures of Love."

01:03:30.220 --> 01:03:31.980
It's a famous French ballad.

01:03:31.980 --> 01:03:33.310
I played that as loud--

01:03:33.310 --> 01:03:35.220
And so the guy
stalked over to me.

01:03:35.220 --> 01:03:39.530
He said, wow man, that's
pretty good music.

01:03:39.530 --> 01:03:41.706
And so I said, well,
you know, we've

01:03:41.706 --> 01:03:43.080
all been listening
to your stuff,

01:03:43.080 --> 01:03:44.895
and you never hear our stuff.

01:03:44.895 --> 01:03:47.020
And so, you never know what
you're going to expect,

01:03:47.020 --> 01:03:51.010
but I don't think we
have to take it anymore.

01:03:51.010 --> 01:03:52.530
We don't have
monasteries anymore,

01:03:52.530 --> 01:03:55.660
but we have them in our mind.

01:03:55.660 --> 01:03:58.780
I go to a Wal-Mart where
everybody is basically

01:03:58.780 --> 01:03:59.390
from Mexico.

01:03:59.390 --> 01:04:00.431
They're wonderful people.

01:04:00.431 --> 01:04:03.040
Most of them are illegal,
but I see things in there

01:04:03.040 --> 01:04:04.120
you'd never believe.

01:04:04.120 --> 01:04:06.570
Five or six EBT cards.

01:04:06.570 --> 01:04:07.335
Everything.

01:04:07.335 --> 01:04:08.710
This is going to
sound illiberal.

01:04:08.710 --> 01:04:10.510
I shouldn't say it on tape.

01:04:10.510 --> 01:04:12.430
But I was in there
yesterday, and there was

01:04:12.430 --> 01:04:16.570
an Asian person who was there.

01:04:16.570 --> 01:04:18.400
And he was watching
everybody speaking

01:04:18.400 --> 01:04:21.250
in Spanish, everything,
and he was very educated.

01:04:21.250 --> 01:04:23.410
And he said to me, Dr.
Livingston, I presume.

01:04:23.410 --> 01:04:25.420
And I thought, what
does that mean?

01:04:25.420 --> 01:04:26.860
And he was Asian.

01:04:26.860 --> 01:04:30.310
And what he was saying
is that I'm Asian.

01:04:30.310 --> 01:04:31.180
You're white.

01:04:31.180 --> 01:04:33.100
There's Mexican-American
people like us,

01:04:33.100 --> 01:04:35.830
but we don't have to accept
the fact that everybody

01:04:35.830 --> 01:04:38.170
comes to the United
States and has EBT cards

01:04:38.170 --> 01:04:41.350
and does not learn the
language and is rude in line.

01:04:41.350 --> 01:04:43.510
And just because it's
a diversity issue,

01:04:43.510 --> 01:04:45.370
we're not supposed
to say anything.

01:04:45.370 --> 01:04:48.490
This person was third
generation Japanese.

01:04:48.490 --> 01:04:50.130
So I think people
are saying, you

01:04:50.130 --> 01:04:51.770
know what, I don't
have to apologize.

01:04:51.770 --> 01:04:57.460
What propelled Trump was the
total absence of any guilt.

01:04:57.460 --> 01:04:59.290
He didn't apologize
for being wealthy.

01:04:59.290 --> 01:05:01.870
He didn't apologize
for being crude.

01:05:01.870 --> 01:05:03.370
A very prominent
editor called me

01:05:03.370 --> 01:05:05.661
and said Trump is going to
pull out on Access Hollywood

01:05:05.661 --> 01:05:08.764
because they released today,
and we need to get on board.

01:05:08.764 --> 01:05:10.180
I said he's not
going to pull out.

01:05:10.180 --> 01:05:11.846
He was crude; he
shouldn't have done it.

01:05:11.846 --> 01:05:14.440
But he's not the
apologetic type.

01:05:14.440 --> 01:05:16.150
Maybe sometimes he should.

01:05:16.150 --> 01:05:17.699
But I think it was
a shout and said,

01:05:17.699 --> 01:05:19.240
we're not going to
apologize anymore.

01:05:19.240 --> 01:05:20.830
We have nothing
to apologize for.

01:05:23.744 --> 01:05:24.910
Deterrence is a funny thing.

01:05:24.910 --> 01:05:27.820
Where I live, most of my
friends are Mexican-American,

01:05:27.820 --> 01:05:30.490
and we have canteens
up and down our avenue,

01:05:30.490 --> 01:05:32.530
and they all have Mexican flags.

01:05:32.530 --> 01:05:37.760
Two weeks after the
election, American flag,

01:05:37.760 --> 01:05:39.800
California flag at every one.

01:05:39.800 --> 01:05:41.750
I go to Wal-Mart parking lot.

01:05:41.750 --> 01:05:44.270
My car has been hit by people
that don't have licenses.

01:05:44.270 --> 01:05:47.300
Came from Mexico, very hard
working, but they hit the car,

01:05:47.300 --> 01:05:48.740
and they leave.

01:05:48.740 --> 01:05:52.597
Woman hit my bumper,
ready to leave.

01:05:52.597 --> 01:05:53.180
I look at her.

01:05:53.180 --> 01:05:54.450
She rolls down the
window and said

01:05:54.450 --> 01:05:55.550
please don't tell Donald Trump.

01:05:55.550 --> 01:05:56.966
I don't want to
go back to Mexico.

01:05:56.966 --> 01:05:58.190
[LAUGHTER]

01:05:58.190 --> 01:05:59.600
I'm serious.

01:05:59.600 --> 01:06:01.700
So, the left would
say that that's

01:06:01.700 --> 01:06:04.520
a sign of the climate of
fear and anxiety in the lives

01:06:04.520 --> 01:06:06.200
that he's ruining.

01:06:06.200 --> 01:06:08.900
A skeptic would say
he created deterrence.

01:06:08.900 --> 01:06:12.460
So I think it's still healthy.

01:06:12.460 --> 01:06:15.020
I know there can be darker
channels we could go to,

01:06:15.020 --> 01:06:20.750
but I think people have to learn
that the guy you see on MSNBC

01:06:20.750 --> 01:06:22.850
or the op-ed that you
read in the Washington

01:06:22.850 --> 01:06:24.830
Post or that
professor on YouTube

01:06:24.830 --> 01:06:28.000
is not representative
of most people.

01:06:28.000 --> 01:06:30.120
And he's actually ridiculous.

01:06:30.120 --> 01:06:32.366
He's an emperor
without any clothes.

01:06:32.366 --> 01:06:33.490
All you have to do show it.

01:06:33.490 --> 01:06:36.390
And that's what they
did in that election.

01:06:36.390 --> 01:06:38.240
[APPLAUSE]

